WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:14.180
[Dan] What you’re looking at is a story.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.610
It’s a story about numbers, it’s a story
about people, and it’s a story about how

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.470
numbers and people interact.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:26.390
It’s a story about World of Warcraft, in
specific, but it’s also a story about every

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:30.660
video game, in a way a story about video games
themselves.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:35.420
It’s a story about why it’s rude to be
bad at World of Warcraft.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.960
It’s December 2004.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.880
Massively Multiplayer Online Role Playing
Game World of Warcraft is taking the world

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.910
by storm and Wallace, the Gnome Rogue walks
barefoot through Molten Core.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:50.260
Wallace, a proper hobbit of a gnome, doesn’t
wear shoes, and he’s not in a hurry to get

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:51.260
anywhere.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.270
His guild thinks this is a little embarrassing,
but it mostly just makes for a story about

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:58.550
a quirky weirdo they met in this new video
game.

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:02.480
Ultimately they enjoy having Wallace around
because he makes the game fun.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.830
Six months later they’re screaming at Wallace
and demanding that he equip some boots.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:11.080
Ultimatums are dropped, tempers are elevated,
and no one’s having a good time.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.340
Someone calls Wallace a bad player.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.310
No one disagrees.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:21.500
[Gentle music]

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:32.110
[Choice] ‘Instrumental play’ was first
described by literary theorist Wolfgang Iser

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:34.930
in his 1993 essay “The Fictive and the Imaginary.”

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:39.570
Iser, a highly influential theorist, saw play
as being divided cleanly into the categories

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:44.170
of free and instrumental based on how the
act of playing related to goals.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:49.780
On one hand is play unburdened by systematized
goals and on the other hand, play with goals.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:54.221
In Iser’s framework play becomes instrumental
the moment it has some intended outcome, something

00:01:54.221 --> 00:01:56.790
resembling rules and structure.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.299
However in Iser’s work this was largely
to distinguish between children chasing each

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:04.159
other around a field for no reason other than
because it felt fun in the moment, and children

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:08.140
chasing each other in a game of football with
concrete winners and losers.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:12.880
Its discussion in relation to video games
is credited to sociologist T.L Taylor in her

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.020
2006 book Play Between Worlds.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:19.750
‘Instrumental play is a goal-oriented approach
that values efficiency, expertise and optimizing

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.840
strategies as part of play.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.920
In instrumental play, the point of playing
is not to reach the end, but to find the best

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.450
way of getting there.’

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:32.129
This is, for the record, not a discussion
of good players as opposed to bad players,

00:02:32.129 --> 00:02:36.489
we are talking about the ways you play the
game in which ‘being good’ is considered

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:37.510
desirable.

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:40.629
This is a critical distinction, and perhaps
if there’s going to be one takeaway from

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:46.140
all this it is this confrontation: the idea
that ‘being good at a game is a good thing’,

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.400
that the correct way of playing a game is
one in which you are good at the game, isn’t

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.040
some natural force of reality, but an imposed
value.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.190
If you’ve ever gotten destroyed in a video
game and felt awful about it, just ashamed

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:03.260
of how hard you got thumped, that feeling
isn’t objective or natural, it is an instrumental

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.880
value that you are imposing on yourself.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:10.849
[Dan] It's common for instrumental play to
be framed in opposition to fun, that they

00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:13.180
are ends of a spectrum.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.739
This is understandable in no small part because
instrumental play tends towards optimization,

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:22.040
which can often result in deeply un-fun player
behaviors.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:26.319
This gets extended out to the extreme where
play framed around challenge or investment

00:03:26.319 --> 00:03:31.920
is treated as irrational or somehow less genuine
than some hypothetically more “pure”,

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.180
“innocent”, “unadulterated” version
of play unconcerned with doing well.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:42.840
It’s important in this conversation to establish,
firmly, that this is a false dichotomy.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.010
We are going to spend a lot of time talking
about how fun gets optimized out of games,

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:51.629
which is why I want to stress that they are
not antithetical concepts.

00:03:51.629 --> 00:03:56.030
Rather than being in conflict with one another
they are instead in tension: there is not

00:03:56.030 --> 00:04:00.340
an opposed relationship, but there is a complex
one.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:04.040
Because if you back up and think about it,
it’s easy to appreciate how someone can

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:09.739
find a deep enjoyment in improving at a skill,
or achieving goals, or discovering solutions

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:11.829
to complex problems.

00:04:11.829 --> 00:04:17.500
Rather, instrumental play, play focused on
goals, can be juxtaposed with free play, the

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.880
form of play that is without ending.

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:25.440
Neither free or instrumental play can exist
in their purest form, but René Glas in his

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.509
book Battlefields of Negotiation describes
play in World of Warcraft as movement between

00:04:30.509 --> 00:04:33.840
instrumental and free play.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:39.110
For every arena match or dungeon, there is
a time before and afterward when you’re

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:43.020
waiting in town and just stand there, aimless.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:47.460
This moment, this is free play approaching
its pure form.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:53.050
An important consideration here is that game
design can rarely compel the player to take

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:54.050
action.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:59.410
A game can attempt to prescribe an action,
such as the famous ‘press F to pay respects’,

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:03.960
but players tend to chafe against moments
like this, as in clumsy hands they tend to

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:10.860
come off as naive or insincere or, in the
language of the youth, cringe.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:15.610
If you refuse to pay your respects then that’s
it, the game doesn’t even end so much as

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:20.639
it just bricks, a standoff between you and
a choice that doesn’t exist.

00:05:20.639 --> 00:05:25.789
Whether or not you close the application,
if you refuse to pay your respects you functionally

00:05:25.789 --> 00:05:29.100
stop playing the game.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:32.580
World of Warcraft offers players a tremendous
amount of agency.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.960
It is, after all, an entire virtual world.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:40.360
You are, indeed, broadly free to not pay your
respects and the game is generally cool with

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:41.360
that.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.680
You can shun levels, talent trees, and even
weapons and still play the game for a very,

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.600
very long time in a way that’s still identifiably
play.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:56.280
Why shouldn’t you stand facing a wall on
the far side of Bloodmyst Isle and contemplate

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:59.590
the nature of the Draenei… forever?

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:06.560
Roleplaying is a genuine example of the value
of free play, and the fuzzy edges of instrumental

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:07.760
play.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:11.310
Roleplayers engage with World of Warcraft
in a fundamentally different way than how

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:13.940
you or I might engage with it.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:18.800
They are often less concerned with the explicit
objectives of World of Warcraft, and instead

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.639
prefer emergent activities.

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:26.430
By shunning the game’s progression system,
there’s a clear embrace of free play here.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:31.220
but when roleplayers self-direct, say, by
holding an annual festival that raises tens

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:36.100
of thousand dollars of charity year after
year, they are creating their own goals and

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:39.970
that morphs into a form of instrumental play.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:45.960
In this sense, you cannot have free and instrumental
play in their purest forms.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:50.229
You might be surprised just how much of the
study of WoW is concerned with roleplaying,

00:06:50.229 --> 00:06:55.110
because it reflects a fascinating alternative,
and socially-driven, means of engaging with

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:56.110
a game.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:07:01.430
And that nuance, that question of how you
can enable a form of play that, by definition,

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:07.210
doesn’t concern itself with, like, the numbers,
the spreadsheets, the statistics, all the

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:12.500
stuff that the game is fundamentally made
of, that is awesome.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:17.460
Blizzard can’t force you to stop staring
at the wall, but they can encourage and discourage

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:23.580
certain play practices and very rarely abolish
them in their entirety.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:28.620
In Battlefields of Negotiation, Glas’s core
insight is that Blizzard is not an omnipotent

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:32.310
god in the World of Warcraft, the developers
are not the Arbiter.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:37.940
Yes, they have immense influence and a form
of final say, but they are ultimately a stakeholder

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:45.139
who push their vision of the game, which either
aligns or resists against the playerbase.

00:07:45.139 --> 00:07:50.860
Writing on Lineage II, Professor of Informatics
Constance Steinkuhler described MMOs as defined

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:57.350
by a “mangle of play”, the push and pull
relationship between game developers and their

00:07:57.350 --> 00:07:58.350
players.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:02.580
Blizzard has access to certain tools and means
of creating the “legitimate” World of

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:07.610
Warcraft experience, they have control over
the servers and decide what bespoke content

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:13.409
gets made and implemented, but they are not
the sole hand attempting to sculpt the game

00:08:13.409 --> 00:08:19.879
MMOs are “interactively stabilized” systems,
coevolutionary, the emergent result of the

00:08:19.879 --> 00:08:24.580
intersection of designers, armed with their
technology, and players, armed with their

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:26.370
own practices.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:30.940
Blizzard has code, the players have codes
of practices.

00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:35.479
Social codes of practice are ‘the tenets
laid down by individuals within the game who

00:08:35.479 --> 00:08:39.589
have no design power or automatically conferred
authority.’

00:08:39.589 --> 00:08:44.990
These social codes are often linked with perceptions
of ‘foul play’ and the ‘spirit of the

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:45.990
game’.

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:50.070
But they go far beyond that, and can shape
what is considered legitimate play in World

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:51.339
of Warcraft.

00:08:51.339 --> 00:08:57.020
In Leet Noobs, Mark Chen sees success in World
of Warcraft as dynamically defined.

00:08:57.020 --> 00:09:01.160
To be good at World of Warcraft is defined
by consensus.

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:06.600
Put another way, you are good if you do enough
of the things that we collectively decided

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:09.130
that good players do.

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:13.230
Success is relative and socially defined.

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:17.769
For a simple example, arena rating was very
inflated in Season 2 of Shadowlands.

00:09:17.769 --> 00:09:24.019
While a typical season tends to have a median
rating around 1600, season one was severely

00:09:24.019 --> 00:09:28.720
deflated with a median rating of 1426.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:35.230
Some behind the scenes tweaking leading into
season 2 resulted in a median of roughly 1750.

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:39.920
In the abstract both of these are fine, the
actual underlying mechanics of matchmaking

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:43.380
are all relative, the numbers are arbitrary.

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:48.050
If you are a high level competitor the experience
on a match-to-match basis is generally the

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:53.450
same regardless of what the median rating
is, the shape of the curve remains the same,

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:55.970
it’s just shifted upwards.

00:09:55.970 --> 00:10:01.269
The hitch is that certain rewards, like titles,
achievements, and armor, are hard coded to

00:10:01.269 --> 00:10:03.260
specific ratings.

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:08.590
So the inflation resulted in the prestige
rewards being available to more players than

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:14.070
usual, many of whom would never have qualified
for it under different circumstances.

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:19.649
This led to a rather vocal belief that these
rewards were illegitimate, they ‘didn’t

00:10:19.649 --> 00:10:20.649
count’.

00:10:20.649 --> 00:10:26.040
Despite the game layer presenting these dragons
as equivalent, there is a significant difference

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:28.260
in their social value.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:32.700
For Glas, World of Warcraft is composed of
various ‘idocultures’, groups of players

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:37.670
linked together by particular systems of knowledge,
beliefs, behaviors and customs.

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:41.780
Big boy raiders and roleplayers have totally
different ways of engaging with the world,

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:45.760
there is no single objective way to approach
WoW.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:47.800
These idiocultures exist in layers.

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:54.711
You’ve got WoW players categorically, PvP
versus PvE, Battlegrounds versus Arenas, Keystones

00:10:54.711 --> 00:11:00.420
versus Raids, Guilds, individual guilds, and
of course cliques within those guilds, and

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:04.760
players can move seamlessly between all of
these different modes, all of these different

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:06.300
ideocultures.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:10.800
These different idiocultures may subscribe
to different social practices, and it is the

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:15.680
interaction between these groups and the constant
negotiation on proper codes of practice that

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.089
Glas argues shapes World of Warcraft.

00:11:18.089 --> 00:11:23.800
It is the interaction between different groups
of people that set the boundaries and fundamental

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:25.880
meaning of play.

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:30.269
Instrumental play has entrenched itself as
the dominant mode of engagement with World

00:11:30.269 --> 00:11:31.519
of Warcraft.

00:11:31.519 --> 00:11:36.420
Through social codes of practice, players
are subject to expectations that they may

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:38.800
be completely unaware of.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:45.040
It is taken as value neutral, and objectively
true, that expertise and success are the natural

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:50.980
objectives of play, and thus the default mode
of play that players owe to those around them.

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:57.370
In other words: It’s bad manners to be bad
at Warcraft.

00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:02.240
[Choice] We’re going to talk for a moment
about the Great Vault.

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:05.912
If you play World of Warcraft then you probably
already know how it functions, and if you

00:12:05.912 --> 00:12:09.480
don’t then all you need to know is this;
at the end of every week you get to pick one

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:13.550
reward from a grid that fills up as you do
specific group activities.

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:15.399
Options are dictated by difficulty and volume.

00:12:15.399 --> 00:12:17.310
Do more stuff, get more picks.

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:20.580
Do higher difficulty stuff, get stronger picks.

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:25.090
Case study A: during the Shadowlands Season
3 race-to-world-first, in the US 2nd guild

00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:30.620
BDGG, our players were required to fill all
9 slots to their maximum potency on multiple

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:31.760
‘mirror’ mains.

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:34.870
Since there is an element of luck to gearing
up, the top players will prepare multiple

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:38.820
copies of the same class in order to have
a few pulls at the slot machine and maximize

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:40.590
the chances of a favorable outcome.

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:45.420
For this particular raid tier, our Moonkin
player prepared three separate Druids, our

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:49.550
Death Knight raider prepared four Death Knights,
each of these characters had the same odds

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:54.290
of being the main character, based hugely
on the outcome of their Vault rewards.

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:59.300
These are players who are paid, in dollars,
for their time as part of a pseudo-professional

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:00.300
eSports event.

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:03.550
Achieving the absolute optimum performance
in a raid environment is part of their literal

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:04.750
job description.

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:07.970
This expectation was only maintained until
the progression period was over, at which

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:11.300
point no one cared, job’s done, do what
you want.

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:16.490
Case study B: Venture, US rank 180th.

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:21.010
They are a 2 night a week guild that has some
very different ideas.

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:24.790
If you’ll allow us this one indulgence,
let’s just take a moment to dunk on an incredibly

00:13:24.790 --> 00:13:26.080
stupid opinion.

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:28.060
If some of this goes over your head, don’t
worry about it.

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:30.550
I promise you will pick up the important parts.

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:34.510
So it’s Season 3 of Shadowlands, almost
3 months into a 4 month season.

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:38.100
Our good friend and contributor to the show,
Crystal, has just come back to the game after

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:42.470
a lengthy break, so we spin up a group and
spend a few hours hanging out and doing dungeons.

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:46.110
In terms of gear, Crystal is wearing old junk
and so the rest of us have elected to bring

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:47.110
alts.

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:50.750
But a member of our group, the tastefully
named Moistrainbow, opts to bring his main

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:55.070
character, in part to make it easy but also
to fill out slots in his aforementioned vault.

00:13:55.070 --> 00:14:00.940
Moistrainbow is doing this for a chance at
one specific item, the coveted Unbound Changeling.

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:04.630
This is one of the best trinkets in the game
for healers and casters at this point in time.

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:08.779
We end up doing over eight keys, but only
about four of them at level 15.

00:14:08.779 --> 00:14:10.930
Moistrainbow does not do any other keys for
the week.

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:13.949
So, at the end of the week, this is what his
vault looks like.

00:14:13.949 --> 00:14:18.960
He has 3 chances at the Unbound Changeling,
two will be at the maximum possible level.

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:23.670
The Changeling is 1 of 91 potential items.

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:24.829
Huh.

00:14:24.829 --> 00:14:28.280
That’s good right?

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:30.580
That’s good.

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:46.430
Having consulted the team, a player getting
their Best-in-Slot trinket is good.

00:14:46.430 --> 00:14:48.269
But Venture felt differently.

00:14:48.269 --> 00:14:51.980
They came down on Moist hard for not maxing
out that final slot.

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:56.180
There was explicit shaming, snarky remarks,
even cowardly announcements from leadership

00:14:56.180 --> 00:14:59.480
about players needing to do their part for
the team.

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:02.339
And perhaps on the face of it, you’re empathetic
to all that.

00:15:02.339 --> 00:15:07.090
But we have been pissed off about this incident
for almost six months, so just give us a minute.

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:11.269
Remember, this was a piece of gear generated
solely for Moistrainbow, this is not typical

00:15:11.269 --> 00:15:14.360
loot drama, there was no alternate home for
this trinket.

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:18.339
The issue was entirely in the missed opportunity
at a stronger version.

00:15:18.339 --> 00:15:22.920
Gear in Warcraft is comprised of stats that
are assigned based on the individual item’s

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:26.170
overall budget, dictated by its slot and by
its level.

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:28.709
These stats all do and mean a lot of different
things.

00:15:28.709 --> 00:15:32.290
So the ultimate unit of measurement we use
to value a piece of gear is to calculate its

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:34.130
contribution to the player’s damage.

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:35.480
How do we do that?

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:38.960
Easy, we build a robot to play our character
perfectly while wearing both items, ideally

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:41.949
a couple of thousand times, and then compare
the results.

00:15:41.949 --> 00:15:46.570
Typically, a higher item level should translate
to a better item.

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:51.089
However, trinkets are not like other pieces
of gear, their item level can be deceptive.

00:15:51.089 --> 00:15:54.170
A trinket lives or dies on its effect.

00:15:54.170 --> 00:15:59.279
So this Unbound Changeling was the best possible
trinket with an item level of 272, down 6

00:15:59.279 --> 00:16:00.680
item level from the max.

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:04.360
If we compare simulation results from a Destruction
Warlock at the time, we can see the difference

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:06.509
is barely perceptible on the graph.

00:16:06.509 --> 00:16:10.040
This particular simulation values the overall
impact at 0.6%.

00:16:10.040 --> 00:16:13.339
Here are some more details on that if you
are interested.

00:16:13.339 --> 00:16:17.470
But we’ve buried the lead on this, because
Moistrainbow isn’t a Warlock, he’s a god

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:18.470
damn healer.

00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:20.490
And healers are a whole different deal.

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:24.709
Healers don’t have the same simple, tangible
benefit from gear as damage-dealers receive.

00:16:24.709 --> 00:16:28.550
For well over a decade, it has been standard
practice for guilds to starve their healers

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:32.139
of gear, on the basis that they don’t really
need it.

00:16:32.139 --> 00:16:36.540
We don’t have any real way to even quantify
this difference for a healer.

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:40.949
While this would not be accurate at all, let’s
be generous and assume it would match this

00:16:40.949 --> 00:16:42.360
point-six percent figure.

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:45.990
This was in the context of a guild that was
9 weeks behind the progression curve, working

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:49.210
on bosses that had been hit with the nerf
bat several times.

00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:54.009
And just for those in the know, these people
were six healing Mythic Lords of Dread in

00:16:54.009 --> 00:16:55.009
early June - like…

00:16:55.009 --> 00:16:57.060
I don’t even have the words.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.040
Gear was not their problem.

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:02.209
So a close friend of mine was bullied and
excluded from this group for not spending

00:17:02.209 --> 00:17:06.410
a few more hours running dungeons to load
the bases for a one-percent chance at a maybe

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:11.199
half-percent increase in performance, where
that improvement was entirely disconnected

00:17:11.199 --> 00:17:13.579
from the problems the group was encountering.

00:17:13.579 --> 00:17:17.480
The reason we wanted to talk about this is
that I sincerely could not imagine a more

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:20.780
petty reason to give someone a hard time in
World of Warcraft.

00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:23.170
Frankly, It's pathetic.

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:26.089
This was not an instrumental practice, this
was a social one.

00:17:26.089 --> 00:17:32.520
Here, the social value of the item level was
wildly disproportionate to any tangible, mathematical

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:33.520
value.

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:40.470
The process of completing the necessary 8
keystones was a ritualistic reaffirmation

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:42.130
of commitment to the group.

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:54.809
The fact that the trinket was worth something
was just a pretense to enforce the practice.

00:17:54.809 --> 00:18:03.030
[Dan] Early attempts at anthropological studies
of virtual worlds were fraught.

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:06.710
Some might look back on them as hopelessly
naive, but I prefer to categorize them as

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:08.080
bad.

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:12.530
Many of them sought to reify online spaces,
to rope them off from reality and paint them

00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:17.350
as a separate self-sustaining world, an actual
destination as disconnected from the rest

00:18:17.350 --> 00:18:21.260
of reality as the moon or perhaps Australia.

00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:26.350
In Coming of Age in Second Life, Tom Boellstorff
conducted a study of user experiences in Second

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:32.179
Life, but critically did not involve the user’s
real lives or any other websites or software

00:18:32.179 --> 00:18:33.760
in his analysis.

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:38.950
The work drew criticism from Alex Golub, who
identified this as a dramatic oversight.

00:18:38.950 --> 00:18:43.520
The virtual worlds of MMOs are not enclosed
worlds.

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:48.260
[Choice] My experience with World of Warcraft
is not limited to the executable file on my

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:49.260
harddrive.

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:52.640
Discord, Twitch, Twitter, Google Docs, they
are all ‘places’ where I can engage with

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:55.340
World of Warcraft without ‘inhabiting’
World of Warcraft.

00:18:55.340 --> 00:18:59.140
I work as a contributor to Skill Capped, an
analyst for BDGG and produce my own YouTube

00:18:59.140 --> 00:19:00.140
content.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:03.691
I might be at the point where I spend more
time doing WoW outside of WoW than I spend

00:19:03.691 --> 00:19:05.110
inside it.

00:19:05.110 --> 00:19:09.679
So we have MMO-Champion, WoWhead or whatever,
the term we will be using for this material

00:19:09.679 --> 00:19:11.260
is “paratext”.

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:14.220
If you’re into a certain, lame, type of
youtube video, “paratext” is a word you’ll

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:15.220
have heard before.

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:18.030
It’s one of those words that gets thrown
around quite a bit, but is rarely given a

00:19:18.030 --> 00:19:19.049
clean definition.

00:19:19.049 --> 00:19:23.030
The concept of paratext was originally conceived
by French literary theorist Gérard Genette.

00:19:23.030 --> 00:19:28.080
Genette saw a book as containing the text
of the book, the primary work, but also containing

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:31.960
other components that were necessary for the
book to be complete but that were not an element

00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:34.090
of the primary text.

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:37.470
You have the actual story of Warcraft: Durotan,
but then you also have a cover, the title,

00:19:37.470 --> 00:19:39.169
a foreword and so on.

00:19:39.169 --> 00:19:42.600
Things that aren’t the ‘text’, but still
influence the meaning.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:46.159
The title gives you some idea of what the
book is going to be about, the illustration

00:19:46.159 --> 00:19:48.030
might tell you what a certain character looks
like.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:57.020
For Genette, all of that additional text required
to make the work complete, was paratext.

00:19:57.020 --> 00:20:03.900
But let’s be real, while this is all interesting,
it’s a bit of a niche.

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:04.900
It’s an acquired taste.

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:09.470
The field of literary academia focusing solely
on physical books is not a growth industry

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:11.260
in 2022.

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:14.330
There have been attempts to apply Genette’s
framework to the internet.

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:15.330
For instance, it could be said that this video’s
title and thumbnail represent paratext.

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:16.330
You saw the video recommended by YouTube and
formed some expectation based on what you

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:17.330
saw.

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:18.669
If you’ve encountered the term paratext
in the wild, it likely wasn’t Genette’s

00:20:18.669 --> 00:20:22.630
definition, but rather the definition attributed
to Mia Consalvo.

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:28.110
Consalvo is a Comms professor who tastefully
and delicately but firmly expanded upon Genette’s

00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:29.110
formulation.

00:20:29.110 --> 00:20:34.280
[Dan] For Consalvo, paratext was any text
that “may alter the meanings of a text,

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:38.679
further enhance meanings, or provide challenges
to sedimented meanings.”

00:20:38.679 --> 00:20:45.450
So paratext are texts that exist to influence
what we think of a separate, primary text.

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:49.429
This is reviews, walkthroughs, pre-release
trailers and so on.

00:20:49.429 --> 00:20:55.059
The material that “surround, shape, support
and provide context for texts.”

00:20:55.059 --> 00:20:59.789
There is a legitimate criticism to be made
that this ever broadening definition is quickly

00:20:59.789 --> 00:21:02.770
becoming so vague as to lose all meaning.

00:21:02.770 --> 00:21:06.190
But for our purposes, this is a blessing.

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:10.360
WoWhead and Warcraftlogs are paratext for
World of Warcraft.

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:12.440
Developer interviews are paratext.

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:15.130
Forum boards and twitter threads have been
called paratext.

00:21:15.130 --> 00:21:21.230
Podcasts, fan art, and machinima straddle
the blurry line of being both texts and paratexts,

00:21:21.230 --> 00:21:26.400
converting the original text into a paratext
that provides context and meaning to the derivatives.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:29.010
AddOns have been called paratext.

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:31.080
Whatever this is, Paratext.

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:35.360
[Crusader] 2 v 3 biiiiiiitch!

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:40.510
Do do do do do do!

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:45.650
Do do do do do do!

00:21:45.650 --> 00:21:50.789
Do do do do do do!

00:21:50.789 --> 00:21:52.510
Trash players!

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:54.220
Trash players!

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:55.220
Traaaaaaaash!

00:21:55.220 --> 00:22:01.010
In most media, a movie or painting is produced,
and then paratext is produced which can then

00:22:01.010 --> 00:22:07.970
act on the dominant text, a particularly incisive
review can persuade you into a given reading

00:22:07.970 --> 00:22:13.179
of the story and themes, or fan fiction can
emphasize the prominence of romantic tension

00:22:13.179 --> 00:22:18.730
that would otherwise appear subtle or even
non-existent, that kind of thing.

00:22:18.730 --> 00:22:23.290
Maybe if a movie gets a sequel, the filmmakers
will respond to what people liked or disliked

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:27.279
and thus the paratext has influence over subsequent
texts.

00:22:27.279 --> 00:22:31.610
It is common for paratext to alter the way
that people talk about something, but for

00:22:31.610 --> 00:22:36.210
the most part that’s where it stops; it
is very rare for paratext to go beyond that

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:39.630
and actually alter a text.

00:22:39.630 --> 00:22:44.790
World of Warcraft, like many online games,
has a bit of a different relationship.

00:22:44.790 --> 00:22:49.710
World of Warcraft is constantly being updated
and altered, there is no static final product

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:54.260
that can sit on shelves and be dusted off
by critics decades later, and since Wow is

00:22:54.260 --> 00:22:59.419
constantly being updated so is a lot of the
talk about WoW.

00:22:59.419 --> 00:23:04.820
The paratext is in a constant state of churn
in lock-step with the game.

00:23:04.820 --> 00:23:08.860
At a basic level, paratext is a form of feedback
for the developers.

00:23:08.860 --> 00:23:12.840
YouTube videos critical of certain changes,
bad reviews of an expansion launch in major

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:18.490
outlets, explosive forum threads or viral
Tweets, the developers and writers are in

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:24.160
an inevitable dialogue with these paratexts,
as subsequent updates to the game will inevitably

00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:30.630
be read as either integration or rejection
of the vision presented by those outlets.

00:23:30.630 --> 00:23:35.000
But beyond that, paratext interacts with social
practices.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:39.020
Paratext emerge from, create and disseminate
social practices.

00:23:39.020 --> 00:23:46.140
And what then tends to be prescribed are ideals
of instrumental play.

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:51.210
When a new strategy or idea is discussed,
people make guides or posts discussing it

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:53.280
and push it out in the world.

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:58.400
That then influences how the game is played
and what practices are considered correct

00:23:58.400 --> 00:23:59.940
and legitimate.

00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:04.559
The expressions of this can be surprisingly
pervasive in really unexpected places such

00:24:04.559 --> 00:24:10.520
as which of two perfectly symmetrical hallways
is the correct path to take.

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:15.419
There is a shocking amount of social friction
that can be generated by looking at two identical

00:24:15.419 --> 00:24:20.570
choices, and picking the one other players
weren’t expecting.

00:24:20.570 --> 00:24:25.659
In The Sepulcher Of the First Ones these two
paths at the entrance are the same.

00:24:25.659 --> 00:24:27.850
They’re literally identical.

00:24:27.850 --> 00:24:32.850
There is absolutely nothing to distinguish
going left from going right.

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:37.669
But for whatever arbitrary reason, Looking
for Raid groups have established an entirely

00:24:37.669 --> 00:24:43.150
unspoken assumption, a rut-like convention,
that you go to the right.

00:24:43.150 --> 00:24:47.490
If you get dropped into a randomly assembled
LFR group and you lead the group down the

00:24:47.490 --> 00:24:49.130
left path?

00:24:49.130 --> 00:24:51.289
People will yell at you.

00:24:51.289 --> 00:24:56.160
These are the foundation of all the small
rituals players engage in as they wordlessly

00:24:56.160 --> 00:24:59.350
navigate a dungeon for the fiftieth time.

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:05.190
These are the rituals that allow players to
just know what their expected role is, why

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:13.450
they are the one expected to stand somewhere
or do something without anyone saying anything.

00:25:13.450 --> 00:25:19.450
Many of these silent ruts are just that: ruts
of habit with no consequence, but the reactions

00:25:19.450 --> 00:25:25.120
get even more intense if there are consequences,
even trivial ones, that can be mathematically

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:29.760
quantified in some way or another as less
efficient practice.

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:34.360
[Choice] In the Plaguefall dungeon, the first
boss, the delightfully named Globgorg, will

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:37.210
summon sets of slime creatures that will make
their way toward him.

00:25:37.210 --> 00:25:40.910
If any reach Globgorg, he will be healed for
a portion of his health.

00:25:40.910 --> 00:25:44.660
Low-difficulty or uncoordinated groups usually
just turn and kill the slimes.

00:25:44.660 --> 00:25:48.070
But at the highest difficulties, the standard
practice is to focus damage on the boss and

00:25:48.070 --> 00:25:51.560
prevent the larger slimes from reaching the
boss with ‘Crowd Control’.

00:25:51.560 --> 00:25:55.039
If executed correctly, this positioning and
strategy trivializes the fight.

00:25:55.039 --> 00:25:57.899
And it’s not difficult, but the risk is
enormous.

00:25:57.899 --> 00:26:02.250
Any breakdown in the management of the slimes
can immediately brick an entire dungeon run.

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:05.809
Upon first discovering this strategy on a
Twitch stream, we became immediate evangelists

00:26:05.809 --> 00:26:07.590
for the Globgorg Gambit.

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:12.370
We essentially became agents of the strategy,
and went on to spread its influence even further.

00:26:12.370 --> 00:26:17.770
To quote Kristine Ask, “theorycrafting paratext
both spread and stabilize theorycrafted expertise,

00:26:17.770 --> 00:26:20.409
co-producing a more performance-oriented playstyle.”

00:26:20.409 --> 00:26:24.230
Put another way, when players theorycraft,
when they come up with strategies, when they

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:28.080
run math through spreadsheets to decide which
option is numerically superior, and these

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:32.070
strategies proliferate through the playerbase,
they don’t merely spread the specific strategies,

00:26:32.070 --> 00:26:37.350
they also legitimize and elevate the act of
theorycrafting itself and push all players,

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:41.750
indirectly, towards a playstyle where optimization
is the correct mode of play.

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:45.649
World of Warcraft is so interesting because
it has lived through multiple generations

00:26:45.649 --> 00:26:47.299
of the internet.

00:26:47.299 --> 00:26:51.870
Because the game is so influenced by paratext,
changes in our capacity for paratext manifest

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:54.080
as changes in the game itself.

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:58.279
As the internet changes how we communicate,
the way we talk about WoW also changes, and

00:26:58.279 --> 00:27:00.409
that fact changes WoW.

00:27:00.409 --> 00:27:04.980
In the game's early life, Thotbott and later
WoWhead were, famously, major resources.

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:08.700
But they were only as good as the information
that users fed into them, and it took time

00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:11.850
for the sites to build up a repository of
information.

00:27:11.850 --> 00:27:16.290
And that didn’t guarantee accurate information,
just the existence of information.

00:27:16.290 --> 00:27:20.230
However, as Mark Chen points out, that didn’t
stop the use of these sites from becoming

00:27:20.230 --> 00:27:21.970
expert practice.

00:27:21.970 --> 00:27:23.520
Actually the complete opposite happened.

00:27:23.520 --> 00:27:29.260
[Dan] Rather than being seen as cheating,
using WoWhead was a thing that the best players

00:27:29.260 --> 00:27:30.260
did.

00:27:30.260 --> 00:27:36.210
And in 2006, it relied on word-of-mouth, you
had to be told about Thottbot to learn about

00:27:36.210 --> 00:27:37.210
it.

00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:41.419
In her book Cheating, Consalvo points out
how common it was for strategy guides or sites

00:27:41.419 --> 00:27:46.360
like GameFAQs to be perceived as cheating,
as ‘illegitimate’ practices.

00:27:46.360 --> 00:27:52.230
So it’s interesting how WoW would invert
that dynamic at a similar period of time.

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:56.600
And as Ask points out, that’s, uh, weird.

00:27:56.600 --> 00:28:01.240
When World of Warcraft released, streaming
video was in its earliest stages and it has

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:07.660
existed through from that into the rise of
HD videos on YouTube through to 1440p livestreams

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:08.660
on Twitch.

00:28:08.660 --> 00:28:14.370
Like, this is actually worth stressing, because
it plays into the way that information spread,

00:28:14.370 --> 00:28:18.940
information asymmetry, has changed over the
course of the game.

00:28:18.940 --> 00:28:28.880
If you were watching a strategy video in 2007,
it looked like this.

00:28:28.880 --> 00:28:34.650
[Music]

00:28:34.650 --> 00:28:37.470
But today, in the sci-fi future of the 2020s?

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:41.590
[Narrator] Now, when the fight starts you’re
going to want to spread and take positions

00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:43.990
somewhat similar to this.

00:28:43.990 --> 00:28:47.169
Now the reason you’re gonna want to be spread
out like this is because Archimonde has an

00:28:47.169 --> 00:28:48.830
ability called Air Burst.

00:28:48.830 --> 00:28:53.270
Now this does 1500 nature damage in a 15 yard
AoE around his target.

00:28:53.270 --> 00:28:57.990
[Dan] In his 2013 thesis, Play to win, Benjamin
Egliston predicted the rise of twitch streams

00:28:57.990 --> 00:29:01.370
as a primary source of knowledge for World
of Warcraft.

00:29:01.370 --> 00:29:07.000
The printed strategy guides that Consalvo
wrote about already feel like a distant memory.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:12.250
In 2022, it’s common for players to seek
out primary sources of knowledge in the form

00:29:12.250 --> 00:29:15.210
of twitch streamers or relevant discord servers.

00:29:15.210 --> 00:29:16.420
[cut to DK VoDs]

00:29:16.420 --> 00:29:20.650
Someone looking how to play a Blood Death
Knight in keystones has access to livestreams

00:29:20.650 --> 00:29:23.929
from dozens of excellent Blood DKs.

00:29:23.929 --> 00:29:27.970
Potentially hundreds of hours of VoDs to comb
through.

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:33.190
Taken in its totality, the effect of all this
material can be somewhat insidious.

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:37.220
When discussing strategy guides, Consalvo
observed that these guides taught young players

00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:43.740
that exploration, persistence and strategizing
were essential to succeed in their given game.

00:29:43.740 --> 00:29:49.909
Essentially, the strategy guides taught its
readers what it meant to be an “ideal gamer”.

00:29:49.909 --> 00:29:54.080
When Choice makes one of his dorky PvP guides
and pushes it out into the internet to wither

00:29:54.080 --> 00:30:00.179
on the vine, it implicitly communicates that
instrumental values are desirable traits.

00:30:00.179 --> 00:30:07.039
The video inadvertently reinforces the idea
that watching PvP guides is a good thing to

00:30:07.039 --> 00:30:08.350
be doing.

00:30:08.350 --> 00:30:14.330
Each video represents a brick in an echo chamber
that carries a subtle, yet deafening resonance.

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:19.370
The value ascribed to these videos turns the
game’s best players into the game’s biggest

00:30:19.370 --> 00:30:20.850
celebrities.

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:27.059
This accelerates the process, as their expert
practices gain increasingly rapid adoption.

00:30:27.059 --> 00:30:31.230
Kristine Ask first observed this phenomenon
over a decade ago.

00:30:31.230 --> 00:30:35.169
Imagine having this ringing in your ears for
so long.

00:30:35.169 --> 00:30:38.650
What would that do to a person?

00:30:38.650 --> 00:30:41.590
[Choice] This robot is a product of paratext.

00:30:41.590 --> 00:30:45.330
It’s an understatement to say that simulations
have been entrenched in World of Warcraft.

00:30:45.330 --> 00:30:49.760
They are a core pillar of the game, and often
are the foundation on which other pillars

00:30:49.760 --> 00:30:50.760
rest.

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:54.169
Best in Slot lists, gameplay guides, even
boss strategies themselves, are built off

00:30:54.169 --> 00:30:58.059
principles that arise from simulations and
the values they carry.

00:30:58.059 --> 00:31:01.890
When referring to the combat logging site,
Warcraft Logs, Egliston said “such applications

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:06.320
are now used casually, reinforcing competition
at all levels of play.”

00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:11.159
“Through the production of paratexts, competitive
players are not only creating emergent tools

00:31:11.159 --> 00:31:15.880
for cultivating tactics, but also repositioning
the skilled player as an influential force

00:31:15.880 --> 00:31:16.950
in game design.”

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:21.230
This state of affairs has become so normalized
as to be seen as value neutral.

00:31:21.230 --> 00:31:26.080
In the case of WoW because of the pace at
which paratext is incorporated into the game,

00:31:26.080 --> 00:31:31.730
either explicitly as feedback or implicitly
as the dialect of design, the barrier between

00:31:31.730 --> 00:31:36.470
WoW and its paratext has become so thin as
to be non-existent, and nowhere is that more

00:31:36.470 --> 00:31:39.220
apparent than the subject of AddOns.

00:31:39.220 --> 00:31:48.580
[Dan] AddOns are, of course, software that
can be run inside of another piece of software.

00:31:48.580 --> 00:31:53.481
AddOns were a major pillar of PC gaming in
the late 90s and early 2000s and they remain

00:31:53.481 --> 00:31:55.929
extremely relevant through to today.

00:31:55.929 --> 00:32:00.960
Many famous titles began their life as mods,
many titles are socially considered unplayable

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:07.270
without modding, some legacy titles are literally
unplayable without them especially as hardware

00:32:07.270 --> 00:32:12.200
and operating systems leave them behind, and
mods are a wonderful tool for budding game

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:17.490
devs to learn the craft by poking around and
fiddling with a completed project.

00:32:17.490 --> 00:32:22.090
Mods are still around, but mod support is
far less pervasive than it was twenty years

00:32:22.090 --> 00:32:23.090
ago.

00:32:23.090 --> 00:32:28.220
Today, if a game has mod support it tends
to be a hugely influential aspect of the game.

00:32:28.220 --> 00:32:33.029
Titles like Minecraft, Oxygen Not Included,
Cities: Skylines, and indeed World of Warcraft,

00:32:33.029 --> 00:32:38.900
don’t see mod support as an extra little
feature, but a core element of their game’s

00:32:38.900 --> 00:32:39.900
identity.

00:32:39.900 --> 00:32:44.899
So while mods haven’t gone away, their scope
is far more limited.

00:32:44.899 --> 00:32:52.429
In 2022 the biggest titles are online competitive
multiplayer games driven by live service monetisation.

00:32:52.429 --> 00:32:57.470
Titles like Counter-Strike or League of Legends
place huge emphasis on their competitive and

00:32:57.470 --> 00:32:58.889
esport elements.

00:32:58.889 --> 00:33:03.899
These are games where the integrity of the
competition is critical.

00:33:03.899 --> 00:33:09.010
Support for AddOns represent an unstable element
that few game developers get involved with.

00:33:09.010 --> 00:33:14.440
In fact, most developers of esport-adjacent
titles have an extremely harsh view on any

00:33:14.440 --> 00:33:18.919
third party software that interacts with the
competitive element of their game.

00:33:18.919 --> 00:33:20.480
There are a few exceptions.

00:33:20.480 --> 00:33:24.059
Team Fortress 2 gives the player the ability
to alter the game’s HUD.

00:33:24.059 --> 00:33:29.399
The power the user is given to change the
UI is pretty dramatic, however they cannot

00:33:29.399 --> 00:33:34.179
add new UI elements, only alter the presentation
of base elements.

00:33:34.179 --> 00:33:39.929
The closest comparison I was able to find
to a WoW UI mod was, like, SimpleRadar for

00:33:39.929 --> 00:33:41.049
Counter Strike.

00:33:41.049 --> 00:33:44.169
This is a major point of departure for World
of Warcraft.

00:33:44.169 --> 00:33:48.900
WoW wants to be counted amongst your League
of Legends and Valorants, and while WoW laid

00:33:48.900 --> 00:33:56.130
a lot of groundwork for the modern live-service
model, WoW wasn’t built to be like those

00:33:56.130 --> 00:34:00.320
games, it was built to be like Everquest and
Dark Age of Camelot.

00:34:00.320 --> 00:34:03.830
Both games from the early 2000s with mod support.

00:34:03.830 --> 00:34:10.330
Mods in that era were significantly less potent,
and the competitive, instrumental play of

00:34:10.330 --> 00:34:16.889
2022 didn’t really exist in Everquest in
2003.

00:34:16.889 --> 00:34:23.480
There is an old allakhazam tutorial on modding
in EQ from February 2003, it lists the following

00:34:23.480 --> 00:34:25.790
hypothetical uses of mods.

00:34:25.790 --> 00:34:30.419
“Mods can introduce new information or functions
into the EQ interface, make things easier

00:34:30.419 --> 00:34:33.060
to use, or make the playing screen more pleasing
to the eye.

00:34:33.060 --> 00:34:36.490
They can turn your cursor into a Nature Walker’s
Scimitar, put a dragon in your spellbook,

00:34:36.490 --> 00:34:40.520
and put every important zone [location] or
tradeskill recipe only a mouseclick away while

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:43.250
you are in-game.”

00:34:43.250 --> 00:34:47.919
These are not what we would today consider
gameplay altering mods.

00:34:47.919 --> 00:34:50.560
They are aesthetic and ergonomic changes.

00:34:50.560 --> 00:34:56.100
These are precisely the kinds of mods that
developers of the time would anticipate users

00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:57.100
creating.

00:34:57.100 --> 00:35:00.040
And the appeal of these mods are pretty clear.

00:35:00.040 --> 00:35:03.530
Users are empowered to sculpt the game to
their own liking, and that remarkable feature

00:35:03.530 --> 00:35:05.480
is almost entirely outsourced.

00:35:05.480 --> 00:35:09.930
Mods offer huge value to companies, who are
the ultimate beneficiaries of the work of

00:35:09.930 --> 00:35:11.180
passionate volunteers.

00:35:11.180 --> 00:35:16.740
In 2003, the developers working on World of
Warcraft were not making a competitive title,

00:35:16.740 --> 00:35:20.600
they were making an Elder Scrolls-Ultima-Everquest
type thing.

00:35:20.600 --> 00:35:26.710
Of course, a few people would inevitably min-max
the systems, like any other DnD descendant.

00:35:26.710 --> 00:35:31.650
But the decision to support mods wouldn’t
be one with decades of consequences.

00:35:31.650 --> 00:35:33.310
Probably.

00:35:33.310 --> 00:35:38.480
[Choice] World of Warcraft in a lot of ways
is a victim of its own success.

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:41.560
Its extensive mod support is a relic of a
bygone era.

00:35:41.560 --> 00:35:45.220
Mods today are more powerful than the founders
could have imagined.

00:35:45.220 --> 00:35:48.660
We can argue whether or not Blizzard should
have locked down their API more during development.

00:35:48.660 --> 00:35:52.119
But ultimately that frames the debate as Blizzard
needing to ‘save us from ourselves’.

00:35:52.119 --> 00:35:56.400
The decision to give mod developers freedom,
though perhaps naive, was rooted in player

00:35:56.400 --> 00:35:59.420
and consumer empowerment, as mod support so
often is.

00:35:59.420 --> 00:36:04.030
It’s what modders created and our eagerness
to embrace them that put us in this position.

00:36:04.030 --> 00:36:07.609
So while this may seem redundant, we want
to take a second to draw a distinction between

00:36:07.609 --> 00:36:10.930
AddOns and, well, AddOns.

00:36:10.930 --> 00:36:15.100
On the one hand we have mods like OneBag,
Aurora, and MyRoleplay.

00:36:15.100 --> 00:36:19.359
Mods that improve the user experience or offer
some new feature without influencing combat.

00:36:19.359 --> 00:36:24.470
I would rope most of the Dragonflight UI customisation
options into this category.

00:36:24.470 --> 00:36:28.960
On the other hand you have BigWigs and Gladius
whose whole purpose is to improve player performance

00:36:28.960 --> 00:36:30.180
in combat.

00:36:30.180 --> 00:36:34.680
These are mods whose creation and use is predicated
on instrumental and expert practices, which

00:36:34.680 --> 00:36:40.090
is to say: these mods exist to provide information
that allows you to improve your performance.

00:36:40.090 --> 00:36:44.200
Since these mods are used explicitly in the
pursuit of an advantage, a conversation about

00:36:44.200 --> 00:36:45.599
cheating and legitimacy naturally follows.

00:36:45.599 --> 00:36:48.329
Even though most players don’t frame this
conversation in academic terms, a lot of us

00:36:48.329 --> 00:36:50.599
feel the subject of legitimacy hanging in
the air, like a nerdy poltergeist.

00:36:50.599 --> 00:36:52.087
The mods we are most concerned with, the mods
with the greatest influence on combat, are

00:36:52.087 --> 00:36:53.087
the ones that assume cognitive load.

00:36:53.087 --> 00:36:56.930
[Dan] The seminal authority on this incredibly
niche concept is Mark Chen’s 2011 dissertation

00:36:56.930 --> 00:36:57.940
Leet Noobs.

00:36:57.940 --> 00:37:03.609
Chen wrote extensively about AddOns and their
influence on raid practices as early as 2006,

00:37:03.609 --> 00:37:08.250
but his insights have only grown more relevant
over time as the influence of AddOns has grown.

00:37:08.250 --> 00:37:13.710
In Leet Noobs, Mark Chen is concerned with
how raid groups become successful, and the

00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:18.910
complex social and technical systems that
emerge in a coordinated raid environment.

00:37:18.910 --> 00:37:23.730
Chen sought to demonstrate how AddOns could
become essential to a raid.

00:37:23.730 --> 00:37:27.790
To begin, Chen develops on Edwin Hutchin’s
concept of distributed cognition.

00:37:27.790 --> 00:37:35.030
For Hutchin, many cognitive tasks can be conceptualized
as activities that can be offloaded onto material

00:37:35.030 --> 00:37:36.030
resources.

00:37:36.030 --> 00:37:40.810
If I take notes on a sheet of paper, if I
jot down the passcode to a door or the location

00:37:40.810 --> 00:37:45.810
of a hidden treasure, I am offloading the
cognitive tasks of memory onto the pen and

00:37:45.810 --> 00:37:46.810
paper.

00:37:46.810 --> 00:37:51.740
The pen and paper are assuming certain responsibilities,
they are not passive things, but are actively

00:37:51.740 --> 00:37:56.810
performing a duty of carrying a cognitive
load on my behalf.

00:37:56.810 --> 00:38:00.790
Chen takes this concept to its logical extreme
and asks whether it even matters whether an

00:38:00.790 --> 00:38:03.960
actor in a given system is human or non-human.

00:38:03.960 --> 00:38:09.530
This concept is called object-oriented ontology,
where everything exists equally, tools are

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:12.000
treated as though they have agency.

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:17.210
My brain and a pen and paper are equally capable
of accomplishing the task of remembering my

00:38:17.210 --> 00:38:18.790
doctor’s appointment.

00:38:18.790 --> 00:38:21.359
There are certainly critiques that can be
made of this framework.

00:38:21.359 --> 00:38:26.010
In the context of notetaking, it could be
argued that the paper merely enhances my memory,

00:38:26.010 --> 00:38:27.980
rather than assuming responsibility.

00:38:27.980 --> 00:38:33.990
But as you will see, certain mods are so potent
at taking on responsibility, that ‘agency’

00:38:33.990 --> 00:38:38.990
is probably the best way to describe it.

00:38:38.990 --> 00:38:42.340
This leads Chen to a lengthy discussion of
actor-network theory.

00:38:42.340 --> 00:38:46.790
ANT posits that the roles and responsibilities
within a ‘network of activity’, such as

00:38:46.790 --> 00:38:51.410
an organization or a raid group, are distributed
across multiple actors.

00:38:51.410 --> 00:38:55.670
And object-oriented ontology tells us that
those actors need not be human.

00:38:55.670 --> 00:39:01.280
And since a raid group is nothing more than
a network of actors, Chen argues that AddOns

00:39:01.280 --> 00:39:04.339
can become essential agents in a raid environment.

00:39:04.339 --> 00:39:09.380
[Choice] So, the AddOns we are most concerned
with are the ones that take on some cognitive

00:39:09.380 --> 00:39:13.400
load that would otherwise be placed on players,
individually or collectively.

00:39:13.400 --> 00:39:17.900
From this we can place mods on a kind of abstract
spectrum of just how much cognitive load they

00:39:17.900 --> 00:39:19.180
carry.

00:39:19.180 --> 00:39:22.460
A lot of mods we just don’t care about,
such as those cosmetic or ergonomic mods,

00:39:22.460 --> 00:39:25.290
because they carry no meaningful cognitive
load in combat.

00:39:25.290 --> 00:39:29.850
On the other end of the spectrum mods like
Gladius, Diminish or OmniBar carry a very

00:39:29.850 --> 00:39:34.000
high amount of cognitive load, because they
desperately want to contribute to combat.

00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:37.940
The smallest example of a useful mod we can
think of, our minimum unit of cognitive load,

00:39:37.940 --> 00:39:39.020
would be the crosshair.

00:39:39.020 --> 00:39:42.640
It’s not uncommon now to see top end raiders
play with a WeakAura that marks the center

00:39:42.640 --> 00:39:45.290
of their screen, representing where their
character is.

00:39:45.290 --> 00:39:48.740
Your character cannot, typically, leave the
center of your screen.

00:39:48.740 --> 00:39:51.680
So strictly speaking, the crosshair is entirely
redundant information.

00:39:51.680 --> 00:39:57.460
It is an actor whose role is to answer a question
whose answer never changes.

00:39:57.460 --> 00:40:01.120
Where is my character, it’s in the middle
of the screen, where it always is.

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:05.470
And yet if we show you footage of a modern
raid, you can see how the crosshair can decipher

00:40:05.470 --> 00:40:06.599
the visual noise.

00:40:06.599 --> 00:40:10.910
‘Where is my character?’ is a simple question
to answer, but having that certainty, not

00:40:10.910 --> 00:40:15.780
having to take the extra split second to find
your dude in the chaos, that is valuable,

00:40:15.780 --> 00:40:18.930
and that task can be offloaded to two brightly
coloured lines.

00:40:18.930 --> 00:40:22.819
In recent years, competitive raid guilds have
made a big deal about the 21st raider, an

00:40:22.819 --> 00:40:26.450
additional player outside of the raid that
coordinates players as the raid’s leader

00:40:26.450 --> 00:40:33.250
via livestreams.

00:40:33.250 --> 00:40:36.570
Traditionally that role was performed by a
player within the raid, but the demands of

00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:40.010
the role have grown to the point now where
a player within the raid is not expected to

00:40:40.010 --> 00:40:42.160
carry that amount of cognitive load.

00:40:42.160 --> 00:40:45.421
The cognitive load of managing the raid has
been outsourced to an additional actor who

00:40:45.421 --> 00:40:47.430
is not on the raid frames.

00:40:47.430 --> 00:40:49.630
However, that idea is not remotely new.

00:40:49.630 --> 00:40:55.650
T. L Taylor, writing in 2009 on the subject
of raiding in Molten Core, framed a predecessor

00:40:55.650 --> 00:41:00.060
to DBM as “a kind of autonomous agent, the
41st member of the raid.”

00:41:00.060 --> 00:41:04.310
It’s quite common for raid mods like DBM
or BigWigs to assign players to certain locations

00:41:04.310 --> 00:41:06.319
or to perform certain duties.

00:41:06.319 --> 00:41:09.990
On the Mythic Jailer encounter, six players
are marked as the bomb, they’re about to

00:41:09.990 --> 00:41:10.990
explode.

00:41:10.990 --> 00:41:13.980
To keep their friends alive, they each need
to jump in a unique hole.

00:41:13.980 --> 00:41:17.900
To minimize the amount of coordination or
communication required, players offload the

00:41:17.900 --> 00:41:19.410
problem to a mod.

00:41:19.410 --> 00:41:23.490
An AddOn assigns each player their own hole,
and your duty as a raider is to do what the

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:24.490
robot tells you.

00:41:24.490 --> 00:41:27.670
By the time this goes live, progression for
Vault of the Incarnates will begin in less

00:41:27.670 --> 00:41:28.770
than a fortnight.

00:41:28.770 --> 00:41:32.599
A huge point of discussion for us has been
how to best utilize mods to assume as much

00:41:32.599 --> 00:41:34.359
responsibility as possible.

00:41:34.359 --> 00:41:37.560
Just how much of our Raid Leader could be
replaced with MS Susan?

00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:41.740
[Dan] In Leet Noobs, Chen discusses at great
length how the creation of threat meters affected

00:41:41.740 --> 00:41:42.740
his guild.

00:41:42.740 --> 00:41:47.540
We take it for granted today, but in early
WoW the threat system was a black box.

00:41:47.540 --> 00:41:51.400
The lack of knowledge around threat meant
that players couldn’t necessarily ‘optimize’

00:41:51.400 --> 00:41:55.880
their damage, because if they did too much
damage they would simply pull aggro and die.

00:41:55.880 --> 00:42:00.190
And you couldn’t put a number to what ‘too
much damage’ even was.

00:42:00.190 --> 00:42:03.390
Chen describes in detail his guild’s theories
on how threat functioned.

00:42:03.390 --> 00:42:07.740
I won’t get into the details, but it was
a vision of threat that had rogues afraid

00:42:07.740 --> 00:42:13.400
to ever press Eviscerate, a core ability that
famously was kind of important, it was worth

00:42:13.400 --> 00:42:15.130
a press.

00:42:15.130 --> 00:42:18.590
“Eviscerate does static damage!”

00:42:18.590 --> 00:42:23.190
Early theories on threat were similar to Onyxia’s
famous deep breath, it was shrouded in half-truths,

00:42:23.190 --> 00:42:27.060
vague uncertainties and a healthy dose of
superstition.

00:42:27.060 --> 00:42:31.240
Threat in 2004 was what we’d today call
a vibe, you felt it out.

00:42:31.240 --> 00:42:35.440
A scenario like this, where DPS are deliberately
holding back their damage due to concerns

00:42:35.440 --> 00:42:44.859
about threat, significantly increased the
difficulty of bosses.

00:42:44.859 --> 00:42:47.690
The superstition was replaced with absolute
knowledge.

00:42:47.690 --> 00:42:53.470
“A mechanic that was previously invisible
but still present was made visible in order

00:42:53.470 --> 00:42:55.000
to be made useful.”

00:42:55.000 --> 00:42:58.770
The process of threat was now a process of
real-time data analysis.

00:42:58.770 --> 00:43:03.230
If the rogue saw that they had a decent buffer
on threat, they could press Eviscerate where

00:43:03.230 --> 00:43:05.010
they would have once hesitated.

00:43:05.010 --> 00:43:08.890
As tools, the damage meter naturally preceded
the threat meter.

00:43:08.890 --> 00:43:13.609
But in the early days the value of damage
meters wasn’t appreciated.

00:43:13.609 --> 00:43:18.560
Chen observed that his guild members initially
struggled to see the purpose or value in these

00:43:18.560 --> 00:43:19.560
tools.

00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:23.540
He invoked the metaphor of ‘trying to describe
a bicycle to a fish’.

00:43:23.540 --> 00:43:27.819
But once they were adopted, they quickly became
essential to Chen’s guild, and the threat

00:43:27.819 --> 00:43:31.150
meter naturally elevates the damage meter.

00:43:31.150 --> 00:43:35.060
Because ultimately, the value of threat management
is measured in damage per second.

00:43:35.060 --> 00:43:39.609
The creation of the damage meter created the
conditions that allowed players to optimize

00:43:39.609 --> 00:43:45.110
their damage output, and thus legitimized
the damage meter as a meaningful signifier

00:43:45.110 --> 00:43:46.450
of contribution.

00:43:46.450 --> 00:43:51.250
The reason why we articulate the value of
gear in terms of damage per second, is because

00:43:51.250 --> 00:43:56.260
DPS is the base unit that we use to measure
value to a raid.

00:43:56.260 --> 00:44:05.090
What was once the central pillar of the DPS
experience was completely changed almost overnight.

00:44:05.090 --> 00:44:09.870
In the years that followed, threat became
a rudderless system, ignored by players unless

00:44:09.870 --> 00:44:11.480
they chafed against it.

00:44:11.480 --> 00:44:16.890
Over the years Blizzard has had a few cracks
at making it relevant again; but as you’ll

00:44:16.890 --> 00:44:18.920
recall, the creators can’t force it.

00:44:18.920 --> 00:44:25.180
In a modern context it is easy to view this
as a positive thing - threat sucks and the

00:44:25.180 --> 00:44:28.060
game is better off without it sitting on top
of everything.

00:44:28.060 --> 00:44:34.500
That may be true, but because nothing with
WoW is simple, there is more to it than that.

00:44:34.500 --> 00:44:38.920
Chen describes a couple of different players
from the early days, each with various quirks,

00:44:38.920 --> 00:44:43.080
but for narrative simplicity we’re going
to merge them into the archetype of Wallace,

00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:48.210
a character who walks between encounters and
doesn’t wear shoes - two real behaviors

00:44:48.210 --> 00:44:49.369
that Chen witnessed.

00:44:49.369 --> 00:44:52.819
“There was one of my guild mates, not guild
mates because we were in a different guild,

00:44:52.819 --> 00:44:55.080
raid mates - since he was in a different guild.

00:44:55.080 --> 00:45:01.410
He played this character who refused to wear
boots, or footwear.

00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:06.480
Every piece of clothing or item you get can
add to your stats.

00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:13.860
So to not wear boots is a handicap, but like,
we didn’t care because he was roleplaying

00:45:13.860 --> 00:45:20.600
and having fun and stuff … And there was
another person who never ran.

00:45:20.600 --> 00:45:25.480
Which is a total pain in the ass, because
you have to wait for this person to take forever

00:45:25.480 --> 00:45:30.020
to get to wherever you're going, but he never
ever ran in the game.”

00:45:30.020 --> 00:45:34.790
The experience of the Wallaces of the world
was to rapidly see the attitude of the world

00:45:34.790 --> 00:45:41.430
shift around them, from light roasting over
silly character decisions to active hostility.

00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:44.329
Threat meters changed what raiding meant.

00:45:44.329 --> 00:45:49.589
To RP walk everywhere wasn’t a cute character
trait, it was leaving the job of trash clearing

00:45:49.589 --> 00:45:50.910
to everyone else.

00:45:50.910 --> 00:45:56.930
Wallace’s lesser contribution could be portrayed
with the objectivity of mathematics.

00:45:56.930 --> 00:46:02.250
This is how social practices change, and how
mods can drive that change.

00:46:02.250 --> 00:46:06.050
Threat meters and damage meters created a
new framework for which WoW could be viewed

00:46:06.050 --> 00:46:13.000
by players, and as an individual, you could
either accept that change or leave.

00:46:13.000 --> 00:46:18.670
Wallace not wearing shoes shifted from being
an self-imposed limitation with very nebulous

00:46:18.670 --> 00:46:27.180
consequences to being a very precisely quantifiable
measure of exactly just how much Wallace was

00:46:27.180 --> 00:46:30.000
failing to Play Correctly.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:35.640
And in defining what it meant to ‘correctly’
play World of Warcraft, we narrowed down the

00:46:35.640 --> 00:46:37.859
legitimate means of inhabiting Azeroth.

00:46:37.859 --> 00:46:43.950
In doing so, the infinite world that so enthralled
those anthropologists grew just that little

00:46:43.950 --> 00:46:50.960
bit smaller.

00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:55.650
A typical single player game may be built
with instrumental play in mind, it may even

00:46:55.650 --> 00:47:01.200
distinctly cater to it, but peak instrumental
practices can often be quarantined from mainstream

00:47:01.200 --> 00:47:02.200
view.

00:47:02.200 --> 00:47:03.380
Think speed running.

00:47:03.380 --> 00:47:07.400
I love Dark Souls 3, but have never seriously
looked at speed running it.

00:47:07.400 --> 00:47:12.440
In comparison to regular play, the speed runs
look incoherent, the two are disconnected

00:47:12.440 --> 00:47:14.950
to the point they’re functionally different
games.

00:47:14.950 --> 00:47:21.220
A rich speed running scene can exist without
it influencing my experience of the game.

00:47:21.220 --> 00:47:22.220
[WoW footage]

00:47:22.220 --> 00:47:27.200
World of Warcraft, in contrast, has a very
different relationship with instrumental practices.

00:47:27.200 --> 00:47:30.930
You can’t really escape them, even if you
try.

00:47:30.930 --> 00:47:35.700
Even the social elements of the game, things
seemingly free of numbers, become vessels

00:47:35.700 --> 00:47:38.410
for the propagation of the numbers.

00:47:38.410 --> 00:47:44.300
WoW is a big game, and it’s extremely complex
and is composed of nearly two decades of sediment,

00:47:44.300 --> 00:47:49.720
long strings of stuff positioned with little
baked in explanation of what it’s for, why

00:47:49.720 --> 00:47:52.560
it’s there, or even if you should bother.

00:47:52.560 --> 00:47:56.250
When new content is introduced no one goes
back to the old stuff to drop signs saying

00:47:56.250 --> 00:48:00.430
“hey, this place is kinda done, you sure
you want to be here?”

00:48:00.430 --> 00:48:04.270
There’s a lot of reasons for that, namely
it wouldn’t even necessarily make the game

00:48:04.270 --> 00:48:08.640
better, but it’s a thing, and as a result
it creates a lot of cul-de-sacs.

00:48:08.640 --> 00:48:12.490
If you stumble into the Molten Front in 2022
it’s kinda left to you to figure it out,

00:48:12.490 --> 00:48:17.050
the game’s not going to tell you that this
content stopped being relevant November 29,

00:48:17.050 --> 00:48:18.050
2011.

00:48:18.050 --> 00:48:22.670
So, given the existence of these cul-de-sacs,
what is always the first piece of advice given

00:48:22.670 --> 00:48:23.700
to new players?

00:48:23.700 --> 00:48:28.030
Join a guild, find people who will help answer
questions and offer guidance, people who are

00:48:28.030 --> 00:48:32.309
up-to-date on the game, who can collectively
remember what things are, and point you at

00:48:32.309 --> 00:48:37.230
what you can or should be doing, who can tell
you that the Molten Front is not only a cul-de-sac

00:48:37.230 --> 00:48:41.559
but also kinda lame and there’s not a lot
of reason to be there.

00:48:41.559 --> 00:48:46.150
Whether it be that kind of spontaneous, organic
help, or the systematized information from

00:48:46.150 --> 00:48:51.460
Elitist Jerks, guilds are repositories of
knowledge.

00:48:51.460 --> 00:48:57.800
Kristine Ask describes guilds as communities
of practice, as institutions that store information.

00:48:57.800 --> 00:49:03.309
They are communities defined in part by what
they do and why they do it.

00:49:03.309 --> 00:49:09.160
From the very start, players are primed to
engage with WoW as a process of adopting certain

00:49:09.160 --> 00:49:10.549
practices.

00:49:10.549 --> 00:49:14.619
World of Warcraft is a game with a large degree
of interdependence, and interdependence is

00:49:14.619 --> 00:49:18.780
always a tricky mix of reliance and imposition.

00:49:18.780 --> 00:49:23.710
In order for you to accomplish your goals,
you may require me to perform a certain task

00:49:23.710 --> 00:49:25.630
to a certain level of proficiency.

00:49:25.630 --> 00:49:32.380
And if I can’t do that, well, odds are no
one’s going to be happy with what happens

00:49:32.380 --> 00:49:33.380
next.

00:49:33.380 --> 00:49:36.119
[Minus fifty DKP]

00:49:36.119 --> 00:49:39.890
WoW’s raiding environment pushes this to
its most extreme.

00:49:39.890 --> 00:49:43.590
As you’ll recall, Chen describes a raid
as a network of actors.

00:49:43.590 --> 00:49:46.950
Like a musical band, sports team or school
project.

00:49:46.950 --> 00:49:51.261
As a scheduled, regular social obligation,
it’s fair to say some degree of ‘success’

00:49:51.261 --> 00:49:56.829
is required to keep players motivated to attend,
no one wants to feel stuck or left behind.

00:49:56.829 --> 00:50:00.920
As discussed earlier, it can be said that
success in WoW is socially defined, what it

00:50:00.920 --> 00:50:05.119
means to be “stuck” depends on where everyone
else is.

00:50:05.119 --> 00:50:10.180
The raid groups that perform the most effectively
tend to do so by treating raiding essentially

00:50:10.180 --> 00:50:14.760
like work, their work defines what success
looks like in the game, and in turn their

00:50:14.760 --> 00:50:20.470
practices become the expert practices as anyone
who doesn’t want to feel stuck adopts their

00:50:20.470 --> 00:50:24.820
strategies, their group compositions, their
add-ons, and their organization, because there

00:50:24.820 --> 00:50:30.020
might be a bunch of different strategies that
do work, but we know theirs does work, and

00:50:30.020 --> 00:50:32.900
if it works it must work for a reason.

00:50:32.900 --> 00:50:36.640
[Choice] For instance, Limit achieved the
World First kill of N’Zoth by using an external

00:50:36.640 --> 00:50:37.690
raid leader.

00:50:37.690 --> 00:50:42.470
That 21st player outside the raid we mentioned
earlier.

00:50:42.470 --> 00:50:46.430
This was not unheard of, but it was not a
common practice until Limit’s success.

00:50:46.430 --> 00:50:49.560
Three years later, it’s a ubiquitous tactic
in the World First Race.

00:50:49.560 --> 00:50:54.200
And our friends over at Venture, the trinket
guys, they love the external raid leader.

00:50:54.200 --> 00:50:58.010
From Twitch streams to full strategy guides,
guilds like Limit create paratext that document

00:50:58.010 --> 00:50:59.010
their practices.

00:50:59.010 --> 00:51:03.290
This paratext not only contains the methodology
to overcome a specific boss fight or fix a

00:51:03.290 --> 00:51:07.190
certain problem, but you’ll recall it also
normalizes itself as a legitimate framework

00:51:07.190 --> 00:51:08.600
to apply to raiding.

00:51:08.600 --> 00:51:12.670
This is a byproduct of emergent play, the
game’s best players have gotten very good

00:51:12.670 --> 00:51:16.070
at finding unintended solutions to the challenges
put in front of them.

00:51:16.070 --> 00:51:19.300
Which means that what they end up doing can
often be in conflict with what the intuitive

00:51:19.300 --> 00:51:20.450
solution would appear to be.

00:51:20.450 --> 00:51:21.450
What the designers may well have intended
the solution to be.

00:51:21.450 --> 00:51:24.540
Over the course of years, theorycrafting has
become so entrenched in the minds of even

00:51:24.540 --> 00:51:27.540
casual players, that it appears value neutral.

00:51:27.540 --> 00:51:31.680
The way we conceptualize a raid encounter,
the terminology we use, the way we judge success,

00:51:31.680 --> 00:51:35.600
it is all informed from behaviors disseminated
from the top down.

00:51:35.600 --> 00:51:39.490
Even when we do see innovation emerge from
relative unknowns, like Restricted’s awesome

00:51:39.490 --> 00:51:44.150
Mythic Jailer strategy, those strategies are
still products of instrumental practices.

00:51:44.150 --> 00:51:48.440
This goes beyond stabilization of theorycrafting
as an abstract value, this is extremely tangible.

00:51:48.440 --> 00:51:49.440
If a critical mass of guilds are replicating
the best guilds, then the behaviors of those

00:51:49.440 --> 00:51:50.440
top guilds have a direct impact on the whole
culture.

00:51:50.440 --> 00:51:52.690
This level of instrumental play creates a
player base that is better and more coordinated

00:51:52.690 --> 00:51:54.390
than they otherwise would be.

00:51:54.390 --> 00:51:59.140
Blizzard can either ignore this and create
content that is trivialized by AddOns, or

00:51:59.140 --> 00:52:01.069
they can presume that they will be used.

00:52:01.069 --> 00:52:04.780
This has gradually resulted in content that
presumes the existence of these tools, and

00:52:04.780 --> 00:52:07.780
thus pushes players towards adopting them.

00:52:07.780 --> 00:52:11.940
We have seen this phenomenon play out quite
rapidly in the game’s competitive PvP.

00:52:11.940 --> 00:52:15.089
Mods that were once prohibited from tournament
play have become permissible and their rate

00:52:15.089 --> 00:52:17.140
of adoption has skyrocketed.

00:52:17.140 --> 00:52:19.099
This had tremendous implications for the game’s
balance.

00:52:19.099 --> 00:52:21.590
The best players, who had a culture of minimizing
AddOn-usage due to tournaments being seen

00:52:21.590 --> 00:52:24.030
as the legitimate measure of success, suddenly
picked mods up and started outperforming all

00:52:24.030 --> 00:52:25.030
expectations.

00:52:25.030 --> 00:52:26.130
Crucially, this legitimized mod usage in a
PvP community more broadly, giving players

00:52:26.130 --> 00:52:27.130
permission to go crazy.

00:52:27.130 --> 00:52:28.130
This resulted in players who could react faster
than before and make decisions with a certainty

00:52:28.130 --> 00:52:29.130
they never previously possessed.

00:52:29.130 --> 00:52:31.660
The game designers sought to offset these
consequences, and the game now demands an

00:52:31.660 --> 00:52:33.910
airhorn whenever a monk casts Bonedust Brew.

00:52:33.910 --> 00:52:37.800
The only solution to the impact of mods is
to rely more on those same mods.

00:52:37.800 --> 00:52:40.050
It’s a feedback loop.

00:52:40.050 --> 00:52:43.550
As the Race to World First has continued to
pursue professionalism, we’ve seen AddOns

00:52:43.550 --> 00:52:45.740
become a crucial element of the process.

00:52:45.740 --> 00:52:49.230
For years now, the contending guilds have
had honest-to-God software developers on their

00:52:49.230 --> 00:52:53.020
rosters to develop specialized software to
assist in encounters.

00:52:53.020 --> 00:52:57.329
“WeakAuras” is a mod that is so powerful
that it’s not accurate to even call it a

00:52:57.329 --> 00:52:58.329
mod.

00:52:58.329 --> 00:53:01.050
It is one part software development language,
one part mod distribution platform.

00:53:01.050 --> 00:53:04.660
It would be easier to list the things that
WeakAuras cannot do.

00:53:04.660 --> 00:53:08.390
It can be used to single-handedly rebuild
an entire UI, it could be the only mod you

00:53:08.390 --> 00:53:09.569
ever use.

00:53:09.569 --> 00:53:11.390
But even that is underselling this thing.

00:53:11.390 --> 00:53:12.390
I would lose my job if I accurately described
what we can do with WeakAuras.

00:53:12.390 --> 00:53:15.940
WeakAuras have become the first port of call
for any problem in high end raiding.

00:53:15.940 --> 00:53:19.760
Applying the principles of cognitive distribution,
the first question in response to any problem

00:53:19.760 --> 00:53:21.849
is ‘can a WeakAura handle that?’

00:53:21.849 --> 00:53:23.900
To which the answer is most often, yes!

00:53:23.900 --> 00:53:28.839
It is increasingly common to see mods form
the spine of strategies for these top guilds.

00:53:28.839 --> 00:53:33.400
Many encounters, such as Mythic Fatescribe
have strategies that rely on WeakAuras to

00:53:33.400 --> 00:53:34.890
effectively execute.

00:53:34.890 --> 00:53:38.990
Since deferring to WeakAuras is the first
choice, it is extremely rare for an alternate

00:53:38.990 --> 00:53:40.190
solution to emerge.

00:53:40.190 --> 00:53:44.119
What is there to gain by digging your heels
in and not using the assignment WeakAura for

00:53:44.119 --> 00:53:45.119
Jailer Runes?

00:53:45.119 --> 00:53:48.540
Since no other alternate solutions exist,
these WeakAuras become fundamental tools that

00:53:48.540 --> 00:53:50.750
are adopted basically universally.

00:53:50.750 --> 00:53:55.290
The same tools designed by and for professional
raiders end up as standard practice.

00:53:55.290 --> 00:53:58.930
A popular observation regarding AddOns is
the way in which they enable or encourage

00:53:58.930 --> 00:54:00.589
surveillance among players.

00:54:00.589 --> 00:54:05.010
A mod like CT_RaidAssist would function if
other players weren’t using it, but its

00:54:05.010 --> 00:54:07.089
effectiveness was predicated on mass adoption.

00:54:07.089 --> 00:54:10.849
In the case of these auras, they require everyone
in the raid to use them in order for them

00:54:10.849 --> 00:54:12.349
to function correctly.

00:54:12.349 --> 00:54:16.140
If you join a raid and don’t have the correct
pack installed, all the little elves that

00:54:16.140 --> 00:54:19.850
tell everyone where to stand during Prototype
Pantheon melt down.

00:54:19.850 --> 00:54:23.980
This has led to the creation of WeakAuras
to ensure you have the right WeakAuras.

00:54:23.980 --> 00:54:28.310
Sepulcher of the First Ones was dominated
by this bullshit.

00:54:28.310 --> 00:54:33.059
[Dan] So to recap, strategies seep from the
top down, the strategies that the best guilds

00:54:33.059 --> 00:54:35.730
use inform the techniques that will see mass
adoption.

00:54:35.730 --> 00:54:40.240
Smart practice for these guilds is to offload
responsibilities onto AddOns wherever possible,

00:54:40.240 --> 00:54:44.319
this results in a critical mass of strategies
that build themselves around these tools.

00:54:44.319 --> 00:54:47.950
Since there is rarely incentive to develop
an alternative practice, tactics grounded

00:54:47.950 --> 00:54:50.530
in these mods see near total adoption.

00:54:50.530 --> 00:54:54.250
These tools rely on all players running the
software individually, so players have next

00:54:54.250 --> 00:54:57.140
to no say in whether they install this software.

00:54:57.140 --> 00:55:01.290
Because this practice is so widely adopted,
Blizzard, quite rightly, develops fights presuming

00:55:01.290 --> 00:55:05.540
these tools will exist, further entrenching
them as mandatory and incentivizing guilds

00:55:05.540 --> 00:55:10.170
to run even more tools to surveil raid members
and enforce compliance.

00:55:10.170 --> 00:55:14.720
And this is how a guild like mine ends up
mirroring the practices of professionals.

00:55:14.720 --> 00:55:18.880
We aren’t pursuing high ranks or looking
to flatter our egos, we just want to enjoy

00:55:18.880 --> 00:55:19.880
ourselves.

00:55:19.880 --> 00:55:23.560
We just want a sense of forward momentum week
to week.

00:55:23.560 --> 00:55:28.130
In Shadowlands, we’ve found ourselves pressing
people to do things they wouldn’t otherwise

00:55:28.130 --> 00:55:34.700
do, whether that’s run keys, install mods,
switch specs, or hell, switch classes.

00:55:34.700 --> 00:55:39.450
It parallels the experiences of Kristine Ask,
who writes that her 2009 guild, “The Gummy

00:55:39.450 --> 00:55:42.390
Wolves”, sought to tempter their instrumental
practices.

00:55:42.390 --> 00:55:47.680
They wanted to do well on their own terms,
without just doing what Elitist Jerks did.

00:55:47.680 --> 00:55:52.430
They mocked the “no lifers” who took the
game too seriously, but yet were continually

00:55:52.430 --> 00:55:57.880
lured toward adopting the ideas and practices
and strategies offered up by Elitist Jerks

00:55:57.880 --> 00:56:02.450
because, well, they work.

00:56:02.450 --> 00:56:07.030
Because that’s the thing about these practices,
they aren’t arbitrary, they exist because

00:56:07.030 --> 00:56:09.010
they solve these problems.

00:56:09.010 --> 00:56:13.309
And if adopting some of these techniques is
what gets your raid group from stalling out

00:56:13.309 --> 00:56:19.339
to comfortably succeeding, that is a trade
that some would be happy to take.

00:56:19.339 --> 00:56:23.880
But for those players who are left behind,
clearly the problem is the game itself.

00:56:23.880 --> 00:56:27.980
The coevolution of WoW hit a dead end, if
you catch my drift.

00:56:27.980 --> 00:56:34.030
This arms race between the devs and the players,
it’s gotten out of hand.

00:56:34.030 --> 00:56:39.049
What we need to do is hit the reset button,
abandon all this nonsense and return to a

00:56:39.049 --> 00:56:40.670
simpler time.

00:56:40.670 --> 00:56:45.109
Surely, surely, that will fix this.

00:56:45.109 --> 00:56:47.180
[Music]

00:56:47.180 --> 00:56:54.770
When I first covered WoW Classic, I painted
a picture of a game whose primary appeal was

00:56:54.770 --> 00:56:59.829
tourism, a chance to revisit a compelling
game but ultimately one without lasting appeal

00:56:59.829 --> 00:57:01.160
to a broad playerbase.

00:57:01.160 --> 00:57:05.470
I’ve received the odd request for a follow
up video every so often, and now seems like

00:57:05.470 --> 00:57:07.549
as good a time as any to indulge.

00:57:07.549 --> 00:57:12.770
In the recent marketing for WoW Classic, Blizzard
has lent in hard on appealing to the nostalgia

00:57:12.770 --> 00:57:14.099
of a mainstream audience.

00:57:14.099 --> 00:57:18.799
The advertisement is aimed first and foremost
at drawing back players who fell out of love

00:57:18.799 --> 00:57:21.460
with the game.

00:57:21.460 --> 00:57:30.339
I don’t know, maybe I was onto something.

00:57:30.339 --> 00:57:35.270
However, this is not how a hardcore audience
conceives of Classic.

00:57:35.270 --> 00:57:38.829
[Choice] Classic is a return to when the game
was more pure.

00:57:38.829 --> 00:57:42.510
For example, a big complaint from the Classic
WoW purists are the difficulty modes that

00:57:42.510 --> 00:57:48.150
WoW introduced to dungeon and raid content,
uh, in the first year of the Obama administration.

00:57:48.150 --> 00:57:49.150
Raids should be meritorious.

00:57:49.150 --> 00:57:53.240
Killing all the bosses in a raid should be
an achievement, and players should be sorted

00:57:53.240 --> 00:57:54.240
from one another.

00:57:54.240 --> 00:57:57.500
Not everyone should be able to finish the
raid.

00:57:57.500 --> 00:58:02.859
As an example, let's use Battle of Dazar’alor
from 2019, the same year Classic WoW released.

00:58:02.859 --> 00:58:06.950
This was widely regarded as an excellent raid
and may be among the best of the modern era.

00:58:06.950 --> 00:58:10.359
Here we can see the distribution of progression
for the hardest difficulty.

00:58:10.359 --> 00:58:12.980
This is where every guild who killed a boss
finished up.

00:58:12.980 --> 00:58:16.620
For instance, Dan’s guild killed the first
two bosses on Mythic and so they’re somewhere

00:58:16.620 --> 00:58:18.200
in this column.

00:58:18.200 --> 00:58:21.790
As you can see, the largest portion of guilds
never killed the second boss.

00:58:21.790 --> 00:58:24.090
Twice as many as actually finished the raid.

00:58:24.090 --> 00:58:28.570
The data tells us a story of the raid, you
can see just how tough the 4th boss was relative

00:58:28.570 --> 00:58:31.750
to the first three by how many people never
made it past it.

00:58:31.750 --> 00:58:34.930
The distribution drops hard after the first
3 bosses.

00:58:34.930 --> 00:58:39.109
This distribution resembles an ideal raid;
sure, Dan and his 2-decade old friend group

00:58:39.109 --> 00:58:44.220
can see some bosses, but at a certain point
it's time for the men to take over.

00:58:44.220 --> 00:58:47.700
This graph isn’t perfect, a Kungen-type
would probably tell you that too many people

00:58:47.700 --> 00:58:49.180
are clearing the raid.

00:58:49.180 --> 00:58:53.670
But it could be worse, compare this to the
Normal mode distribution of the raid.

00:58:53.670 --> 00:58:57.030
Normal mode represents everything that the
classic purists hate about the modern game.

00:58:57.030 --> 00:59:00.560
It’s a version of the raid intended for
any group to be able to finish with the bare

00:59:00.560 --> 00:59:02.220
minimum of organization.

00:59:02.220 --> 00:59:05.599
We can see from the data that the vast majority
of guilds who entered normal mode finished

00:59:05.599 --> 00:59:06.599
it.

00:59:06.599 --> 00:59:09.450
This number is pathetic, it fails to distribute
players at all.

00:59:09.450 --> 00:59:12.190
No matter your skill level you can expect
to clear normal mode.

00:59:12.190 --> 00:59:16.980
A distribution like this, in the context of
instrumental play, is terrifying.

00:59:16.980 --> 00:59:21.640
What does an objective-driven playstyle look
like when the explicit objectives of the game

00:59:21.640 --> 00:59:23.550
can’t provide an appropriate goal?

00:59:23.550 --> 00:59:27.569
Sure, some portion of this stack are well-serviced
by this raid, but some unknown number would

00:59:27.569 --> 00:59:29.930
be disengaged by this because it’s just
too easy.

00:59:29.930 --> 00:59:34.000
What would the game turn into for those players
if they didn’t have difficulty-appropriate

00:59:34.000 --> 00:59:35.000
content?

00:59:35.000 --> 00:59:37.619
A distribution like this is deeply unhealthy
for the game.

00:59:37.619 --> 00:59:40.079
And it’s the reason why WoW’s early raids
were so difficul-

00:59:40.079 --> 00:59:47.430
I mean, that’s fucking Molten Core man,
it’s the first raid they ever made, of course

00:59:47.430 --> 00:59:50.680
it’s going to be eas-

00:59:50.680 --> 00:59:52.309
Uh, Dan, tag in.

00:59:52.309 --> 00:59:57.630
[Dan] A lot of ink has been spilled on why
Classic hasn’t delivered on the difficulty

00:59:57.630 --> 00:59:59.500
promised by those early evangelists.

00:59:59.500 --> 01:00:02.549
We won't relitigate that in too much detail
here.

01:00:02.549 --> 01:00:07.691
In short, players are better, their internet
is better and for practical reasons the game

01:00:07.691 --> 01:00:11.950
runs on the final build of the expansion,
so player characters end up being stronger

01:00:11.950 --> 01:00:13.539
than they ought to be.

01:00:13.539 --> 01:00:16.690
But the big one is the presence of paratext.

01:00:16.690 --> 01:00:21.500
It’s not just that the content is 12 years
old, and everything has been solved, it’s

01:00:21.500 --> 01:00:26.309
that the solution has been widely disseminated
and widely adopted.

01:00:26.309 --> 01:00:32.650
Like, here’s a taste of a 3-hour lecture
on changes to Shaman going from Vanilla to

01:00:32.650 --> 01:00:34.010
the Burning Crusade.

01:00:34.010 --> 01:00:36.020
Please, I insist.

01:00:36.020 --> 01:00:40.430
“The testings of spell coefficients and
weapon imbues I present here were done using

01:00:40.430 --> 01:00:44.980
a 2.4.3 TBC repack which is not an official
Blizzard product.

01:00:44.980 --> 01:00:48.980
Despite this almost all spell coefficients
and weapon imbue results corroborate what

01:00:48.980 --> 01:00:52.170
has been tested by multiple TBC-era theorycrafters.

01:00:52.170 --> 01:00:57.560
However, until an official pre-patch, beta,
and/or live TBC game has been released, I

01:00:57.560 --> 01:01:02.339
cannot be 100% sure the spell coefficients
and weapon imbue results are completely correct.”

01:01:02.339 --> 01:01:08.670
Yeah, we were always going to demolish Classic,
it was always going to go this way.

01:01:08.670 --> 01:01:13.609
This is not to say that a Sunwell or Ulduar
raid is an unenjoyable experience for modern

01:01:13.609 --> 01:01:19.579
players familiar with the game, just that
it is not strictly a challenging one.

01:01:19.579 --> 01:01:25.960
The issue Classic faces is that the vast majority
of players are exhausting the content at an

01:01:25.960 --> 01:01:29.520
absurd pace, most within the first week or
two.

01:01:29.520 --> 01:01:33.920
These are people who love the game dearly,
and are magnitudes better than the creators

01:01:33.920 --> 01:01:35.299
could have imagined.

01:01:35.299 --> 01:01:38.460
What are they supposed to do to fill the time?

01:01:38.460 --> 01:01:43.400
The same thing as anyone else who has a deep
love of a single game, they started speed

01:01:43.400 --> 01:01:44.430
running.

01:01:44.430 --> 01:01:48.700
Speed running has been an element of WoW for
basically the game’s entire life.

01:01:48.700 --> 01:01:54.150
But before Classic WoW released, private servers
took it to a whole other level, these servers

01:01:54.150 --> 01:01:58.309
would spin up and shut down so rapidly, that
players developed techniques for speed running

01:01:58.309 --> 01:02:00.230
the entire game.

01:02:00.230 --> 01:02:04.230
Those players and their imitators applied
their techniques to Classic WoW at launch,

01:02:04.230 --> 01:02:10.160
Molten Core was cleared in just 6 days, down
from 154 days back in 2005.

01:02:10.160 --> 01:02:13.730
This was a big story in the Classic circles,
but it didn’t really affect the tourists

01:02:13.730 --> 01:02:15.490
in a meaningful way at launch.

01:02:15.490 --> 01:02:19.681
Over the life of Classic, more and more players
found themselves adopting more and more of

01:02:19.681 --> 01:02:20.760
these speedrun techniques.

01:02:20.760 --> 01:02:24.930
Maybe it was an organic process, people just
looking to make their raids easier and find

01:02:24.930 --> 01:02:26.299
a way to make them more engaging.

01:02:26.299 --> 01:02:30.880
But I would suggest that instrumental play
compels players to try and organize themselves

01:02:30.880 --> 01:02:32.160
by hierarchy.

01:02:32.160 --> 01:02:37.700
Remember, the elitist players who want to
gatekeep content, they’re in here somewhere.

01:02:37.700 --> 01:02:41.200
So they are naturally going to elevate means
of separating themselves from the pack.

01:02:41.200 --> 01:02:45.600
So speed runs practices became expert practices,
and, well, over the course of years, that

01:02:45.600 --> 01:02:46.829
cooks your brain.

01:02:46.829 --> 01:02:50.279
Classic ought to have been our salvation from
all this, after all, the content doesn’t

01:02:50.279 --> 01:02:52.990
demand the kind of optimization we’ve been
discussing, not by a long shot.

01:02:52.990 --> 01:02:53.990
Classic should rightly be the playground of
the super-casual, a sandbox for players who

01:02:53.990 --> 01:02:54.990
aren’t in any particular hurry to be anywhere.

01:02:54.990 --> 01:02:57.910
And yet, it's gone the other way, Wrath of
the Lich King Classic has become a nightmare

01:02:57.910 --> 01:02:59.049
of instrumental practices.

01:02:59.049 --> 01:03:03.599
Yes, it’s not difficult, but the difficulty
isn’t the appeal, instead it’s about going

01:03:03.599 --> 01:03:05.990
through this process perfectly.

01:03:05.990 --> 01:03:09.280
To be good at Classic is to learn and adopt
the right practices.

01:03:09.280 --> 01:03:13.119
To play the right class as the right race,
with the best professions; to level the quickest,

01:03:13.119 --> 01:03:16.630
get server first, get the Shadowmourne you
never got.

01:03:16.630 --> 01:03:18.799
In some cases, it seems really weird.

01:03:18.799 --> 01:03:20.640
It’s like reliving high school.

01:03:20.640 --> 01:03:24.970
You’re going to pick better electives, be
cooler, bet on the Cubs in 2016, and smash

01:03:24.970 --> 01:03:26.329
when you've got the chance.

01:03:26.329 --> 01:03:30.829
At the launch of Classic, these practices
existed, but they were largely invisible.

01:03:30.829 --> 01:03:34.700
By definition, it did not mean anything to
you that some guys were in Molten Core while

01:03:34.700 --> 01:03:36.750
you were in Wailing Caverns.

01:03:36.750 --> 01:03:37.750
Whatever.

01:03:37.750 --> 01:03:43.039
But after you quit Classic at level 47, these
players stuck around.

01:03:43.039 --> 01:03:48.039
And these players established the practices
of what it meant to play Classic.

01:03:48.039 --> 01:03:52.289
This process of riding the curve to the greatest
extent possible, it’s not the exception,

01:03:52.289 --> 01:03:53.539
it’s the rule.

01:03:53.539 --> 01:03:55.369
Again, look at these distributions.

01:03:55.369 --> 01:04:00.579
No child is being left behind here, if you’re
entering the raid, you’re finishing it.

01:04:00.579 --> 01:04:04.840
Returning to Wrath Classic in late 2022, there
is a palpable feeling that you’ve walked

01:04:04.840 --> 01:04:06.680
into their space.

01:04:06.680 --> 01:04:11.040
A walk around Dalaran showcases this, every
Death Knight and their ghoul is decked out

01:04:11.040 --> 01:04:16.410
in the best gear, the most prestigious vanity
items, every player looks like the protagonist.

01:04:16.410 --> 01:04:18.430
Things that were once status symbols are mundane.

01:04:18.430 --> 01:04:22.309
The best gear is not a symbol of exceptionalism,
it’s a symbol of parity.

01:04:22.309 --> 01:04:25.520
Having less than the best makes you less than
everyone else.

01:04:25.520 --> 01:04:29.000
Because if you aren’t here, where else can
you be?

01:04:29.000 --> 01:04:32.789
This was made crystal clear during the launch
of Wrath Classic, when players put absurd

01:04:32.789 --> 01:04:35.600
gear requirements on groups for Utgarde Keep.

01:04:35.600 --> 01:04:39.930
If you wanted to experience Utgarde Keep,
the famously easy introductory dungeon, on

01:04:39.930 --> 01:04:44.910
the first day of Wrath, a majority of groups
expected you to have the best gear possible

01:04:44.910 --> 01:04:49.690
Choice thought it was just his sweaty server,
but no, YouTuber AzAMOuS parodied this phenomenon

01:04:49.690 --> 01:04:53.119
and the comment section is full of accounts
of similar experiences.

01:04:53.119 --> 01:04:57.270
“Madorc you are noy good geared enough for
this dungeon.

01:04:57.270 --> 01:04:58.510
Wot?

01:04:58.510 --> 01:04:59.880
Is he joking?”

01:04:59.880 --> 01:05:03.290
Like our trinket from earlier, it wasn’t
actually about efficiency.

01:05:03.290 --> 01:05:06.530
There is a limit on how many times you can
do a dungeon within a given time.

01:05:06.530 --> 01:05:11.140
It was not about efficiency, it was about
reinforcing the ingroup and excluding the

01:05:11.140 --> 01:05:12.140
outgroup.

01:05:12.140 --> 01:05:15.289
The instrumental practices of Classic are
no longer invisible, they’re in-your-face,

01:05:15.289 --> 01:05:17.510
they’re the status quo.

01:05:17.510 --> 01:05:21.859
[Choice] It is no doubt possible for a casual
guild of 40-somethings to have fun on Classic,

01:05:21.859 --> 01:05:26.890
but like Kristine Ask’s guild back in 2009,
these practices are endemic to Classic.

01:05:26.890 --> 01:05:31.339
There is no ivory tower for the best players
to escape to, you all want to do Naxxramas

01:05:31.339 --> 01:05:32.339
this week.

01:05:32.339 --> 01:05:36.049
I would argue that casual players feel more
out of place on Classic, because they disrupt

01:05:36.049 --> 01:05:40.490
an otherwise seamless flow of knowledgeable
and overpowered players blowing through trivial

01:05:40.490 --> 01:05:41.490
content.

01:05:41.490 --> 01:05:42.490
You are their Wallace, bumbling into their
Normal Utgarde Keep.

01:05:42.490 --> 01:05:43.900
This is the dissonance of the marketing of
Classic.

01:05:43.900 --> 01:05:48.859
Blizzard wants Classic to be inviting to returning
players, a chance to return to a humble era.

01:05:48.859 --> 01:05:51.990
Of course this is bullshit, Wrath was the
expansion that gave us GearScore, which if

01:05:51.990 --> 01:05:55.700
you want to talk about mods influencing social
practices, there’s a doozy for you.

01:05:55.700 --> 01:06:00.750
The social experience of Classic is immensely
frustrating and incredibly fucking stressful.

01:06:00.750 --> 01:06:04.230
Once you step beyond the leveling experience,
every dungeon or raid is a ritual of actions

01:06:04.230 --> 01:06:07.089
that has been accepted as standard and performed
without introduction.

01:06:07.089 --> 01:06:11.160
It’s disorientating to have ancient dungeons
being done with private server routes.

01:06:11.160 --> 01:06:15.089
Stuff you were meant to learn 7 years ago
from a Russian Hunter on Warmane’s 7x experience

01:06:15.089 --> 01:06:16.990
BlizzLike pre-nerf Wrath server.

01:06:16.990 --> 01:06:21.380
There is a tangible sense that you are always
behind the curve, always missing something.

01:06:21.380 --> 01:06:25.329
I was so excited for Classic TBC, before I
learned that a bunch of gear from vanilla

01:06:25.329 --> 01:06:29.550
content was on the Best-in-Slot list, like
the Mark of the Champion that was used all

01:06:29.550 --> 01:06:30.859
the way into Sunwell.

01:06:30.859 --> 01:06:35.860
It wasn’t that I needed it to kill Brutallus,
but it was the knowledge that I was already

01:06:35.860 --> 01:06:36.860
behind.

01:06:36.860 --> 01:06:40.960
Classic pushes you into one of two archetypes,
either buy in and end up here, or you reject

01:06:40.960 --> 01:06:44.049
all this, and embrace the identity of a tourist.

01:06:44.049 --> 01:06:48.720
The hardcore players congregate on the same
handful of servers, the casual players find

01:06:48.720 --> 01:06:52.250
their servers starting to die and eventually
either need to transfer to those same super

01:06:52.250 --> 01:06:56.990
realms, or endure their degrading server until
it eventually becomes unplayable and the player

01:06:56.990 --> 01:06:58.029
drops the game altogether.

01:06:58.029 --> 01:07:01.760
In other words, it’s just like fucking Retail.

01:07:01.760 --> 01:07:05.190
Despite the promise of a journey to a better
yesterday, we ultimately recreated the same

01:07:05.190 --> 01:07:07.450
environment as the main game.

01:07:07.450 --> 01:07:10.650
Only there is no border to delineate between
the intensity of content.

01:07:10.650 --> 01:07:16.250
For example, BDGG are a pseudo-professional
guild, our raiders are paid, in dollars, to

01:07:16.250 --> 01:07:20.039
play WoW and perform their best in the raid
content, they have a lot of expectations to

01:07:20.039 --> 01:07:23.410
meet and our analyst team has been hard at
work discussing what needs to be done in preparation

01:07:23.410 --> 01:07:24.690
for this upcoming event.

01:07:24.690 --> 01:07:28.430
One conversation that has not come up at all
is the leveling process.

01:07:28.430 --> 01:07:32.630
Blizzard has had years to separate leveling
from the hardcore elements of the game.

01:07:32.630 --> 01:07:36.339
Our raiders need to get a certain number of
characters to make level in the first week,

01:07:36.339 --> 01:07:39.250
and some are genuinely going to blitz it,
because it’s in their nature.

01:07:39.250 --> 01:07:43.079
But for our purposes, there is no need to
prescribe a method to them or push them to

01:07:43.079 --> 01:07:44.650
get it done quickly.

01:07:44.650 --> 01:07:48.569
They are allowed to take their time, react
to the cinematics with their viewers, explore,

01:07:48.569 --> 01:07:49.960
goof off, whatever.

01:07:49.960 --> 01:07:54.140
Many will experience the leveling of Dragonflight
entirely organically, like you would, or as

01:07:54.140 --> 01:07:55.310
I will.

01:07:55.310 --> 01:07:59.400
By contrast, check out this selection of the
guides on speed running to max level in Classic,

01:07:59.400 --> 01:08:02.770
there is a whole industry built on selling
you instrumental practices.

01:08:02.770 --> 01:08:06.500
The funniest thing I saw in Classic were these
guys who called themselves Team Supreme.

01:08:06.500 --> 01:08:12.240
So, for context here, I joined a kind of middling
guild on a middling PvE server for Classic,

01:08:12.240 --> 01:08:15.819
and I really, I wanted to sort of strike that
balance between hardcore and casual play.

01:08:15.819 --> 01:08:21.940
I wanted to do well and sort of skip the failure
portion, but not have to go too hard.

01:08:21.940 --> 01:08:27.239
And within this guild were these guys, Team
Supreme, and they were set on being the first

01:08:27.239 --> 01:08:30.659
people on the server to get to level 70 in
the Burning Crusade.

01:08:30.659 --> 01:08:34.830
So I approach them out of sincere curiosity
and ask them “hey, you know, what’s the

01:08:34.830 --> 01:08:35.830
plan?”

01:08:35.830 --> 01:08:40.640
And they tell me that they want to do 60 to
70 in twenty hours.

01:08:40.640 --> 01:08:46.160
And that’s a… that’s a complicated number
to have picked, twenty hours.

01:08:46.160 --> 01:08:50.770
If you’re a professional speedrunner, if
you’re Joker, and that is his name, his

01:08:50.770 --> 01:08:54.589
name’s Joker, if you’re Joker and you
do this for a living, twenty hours is a pretty

01:08:54.589 --> 01:08:55.589
doable number.

01:08:55.589 --> 01:08:59.810
But I picked these guys because they were
a middling guild, I picked them because I

01:08:59.810 --> 01:09:02.270
didn’t want them to have heard of Joker.

01:09:02.270 --> 01:09:08.850
So to see so much energy being funneled into
these five guys to do this one activity, and

01:09:08.850 --> 01:09:12.830
to hear twenty hours out of them, I knew they’d
made a mistake somewhere.

01:09:12.830 --> 01:09:13.830
To be honest.

01:09:13.830 --> 01:09:19.659
Not to be rude, but to hear twenty hours out
of these guys, okay, a mistake’s been made.

01:09:19.659 --> 01:09:24.060
I became obsessed with this because in a way
it was like “I’m I the one out of touch,

01:09:24.060 --> 01:09:29.960
have these guys pushed this further than I
could possibly ever have imagined, or have

01:09:29.960 --> 01:09:31.500
they made this crucial mistake.”

01:09:31.500 --> 01:09:37.640
Seeing all this work juxtaposed with what
I thought was just an ungodly number I had

01:09:37.640 --> 01:09:39.250
to know what was going on.

01:09:39.250 --> 01:09:43.710
So I ask them “hey, have you been on the
beta, have you tested this strategy?”

01:09:43.710 --> 01:09:45.450
To which, to my astonishment, they said yes.

01:09:45.450 --> 01:09:50.060
They log onto the beta, turn in all the pre-quests,
that process takes a full hour and nets them

01:09:50.060 --> 01:09:51.650
one and a half levels.

01:09:51.650 --> 01:09:54.670
Which is, that’s a really good outcome,
that is solid stuff.

01:09:54.670 --> 01:09:57.530
And so at the end of the first hour they’re
doing well, looking really good.

01:09:57.530 --> 01:10:01.310
They then proceed to run dungeons for the
next nine hours straight, so by the tenth

01:10:01.310 --> 01:10:03.350
hour they are at level 65.

01:10:03.350 --> 01:10:04.350
The half-way mark.

01:10:04.350 --> 01:10:10.041
At which point they’re not going to do the
full 20 hours of dry run on the beta, so they

01:10:10.041 --> 01:10:11.041
log off.

01:10:11.041 --> 01:10:16.060
Because it logically follows having just done
60 to 65 in ten hours, ten more hours of dungeon

01:10:16.060 --> 01:10:29.550
running will get them to level 70.

01:10:29.550 --> 01:10:32.360
Fuck.

01:10:32.360 --> 01:10:37.679
At times like this, people show you their
true colors.

01:10:37.679 --> 01:10:42.831
I could have stopped them, I had multiple
opportunities to stop them, but I wanted it

01:10:42.831 --> 01:10:45.390
to see this train reach its destination.

01:10:45.390 --> 01:10:48.120
I was revealed to be an asshole.

01:10:48.120 --> 01:10:49.199
But them?

01:10:49.199 --> 01:10:53.590
They streamed the whole thing, and when the
penny finally dropped, they stuck together.

01:10:53.590 --> 01:10:58.400
They were deeply miserable, but they didn’t
point fingers and they didn’t yell, instead

01:10:58.400 --> 01:11:03.800
they pushed through it, they didn’t sleep;
20 hours became 37.

01:11:03.800 --> 01:11:08.310
But they got what they wanted - they were
the first group on the server to hit max level,

01:11:08.310 --> 01:11:14.440
they even finished two raids before anyone
else.

01:11:14.440 --> 01:11:23.489
They won.

01:11:23.489 --> 01:11:28.580
They had months to pick up on their mistake,
what business did Team Supreme have trying

01:11:28.580 --> 01:11:30.950
to replicate the practices of speedrunners?

01:11:30.950 --> 01:11:33.110
Well, because the internet told them to.

01:11:33.110 --> 01:11:35.250
What else were they going to do?

01:11:35.250 --> 01:11:39.630
As players, the push for Classic was in the
name of recapturing an essence of the game

01:11:39.630 --> 01:11:44.350
that was lost, and Blizzard played their part
- they gave us the executable file.

01:11:44.350 --> 01:11:47.540
But it ultimately failed, because we brought
the bug back with us.

01:11:47.540 --> 01:11:51.420
We brought back the paratext industry that
sells solutions, we brought back the practices

01:11:51.420 --> 01:11:56.250
that trivialized content, and when we had
nothing to do, we made a leaderboard out of

01:11:56.250 --> 01:11:58.159
our day to day experiences.

01:11:58.159 --> 01:12:02.060
Shout out to Rooster Juice and their 9:57
Heroic Nexus time.

01:12:02.060 --> 01:12:06.570
What a legend.

01:12:06.570 --> 01:12:12.440
[Dan] This discussion has been dominated by
sociology and anthropology.

01:12:12.440 --> 01:12:16.630
We have talked to death about WoW as a series
of behaviors and trends.

01:12:16.630 --> 01:12:21.670
But no one who came to WoW in its early years
was drawn to it by the promise of adopting

01:12:21.670 --> 01:12:23.670
a series of social practices.

01:12:23.670 --> 01:12:28.949
The genre of virtual worlds are defined first
and first foremost by just that - the world.

01:12:28.949 --> 01:12:31.520
We can see that in how Azeroth is regarded.

01:12:31.520 --> 01:12:38.330
It is considered the main character of WoW.

01:12:38.330 --> 01:12:47.650
Sure, we can make fun of the many self-inserts
and over-played characters, but at the root

01:12:47.650 --> 01:12:49.000
it is the World of Warcraft.

01:12:49.000 --> 01:12:50.730
[Metzen quote from LFG - just says Azeroth
is the main character]

01:12:50.730 --> 01:12:56.020
For many players in 2005, possibly even most
players, the persistence of Azeroth and all

01:12:56.020 --> 01:13:00.130
that came with it, the day/night cycles, the
weather, the scheduled events, the ability

01:13:00.130 --> 01:13:05.290
to walk in one direction for two straight
hours and experience not a parade of procedurally

01:13:05.290 --> 01:13:10.460
generated noise, but hand-crafted vistas,
we had experienced nothing like it.

01:13:10.460 --> 01:13:15.060
The world of Azeroth is beautiful.

01:13:15.060 --> 01:13:30.980
The raid experience is a different story.

01:13:30.980 --> 01:13:35.060
Raids in World of Warcraft can be severely
taxing on hardware, especially for weaker

01:13:35.060 --> 01:13:36.060
machines.

01:13:36.060 --> 01:13:39.880
This was keenly true in the game’s early
years when hardware was both less powerful

01:13:39.880 --> 01:13:42.630
and raid groups involved significantly more
players.

01:13:42.630 --> 01:13:46.520
It was often necessary to lower graphical
settings to improve hardware performance just

01:13:46.520 --> 01:13:47.780
to make the game playable.

01:13:47.780 --> 01:13:52.760
Or if you were Choice, and 13, be completely
oblivious to that and raid at 2 frames per

01:13:52.760 --> 01:13:53.760
second.

01:13:53.760 --> 01:13:54.760
Incredible.

01:13:54.760 --> 01:13:59.020
Obviously, Choice’s performance was hindered
by his single digit framerate.

01:13:59.020 --> 01:14:02.560
Altering your graphical settings would improve
the game performance and result in better

01:14:02.560 --> 01:14:06.850
game performance, a better experience, a more
playable game.

01:14:06.850 --> 01:14:12.290
This line of thinking opens the doors to manipulating
the graphical settings for efficient play.

01:14:12.290 --> 01:14:15.140
[Choice] A simple example would be something
like this.

01:14:15.140 --> 01:14:18.460
This quest involves collecting bottles that
are obscured amongst the grass.

01:14:18.460 --> 01:14:23.140
You can either search the grass carefully
for the tiny little bastard bottles, or you

01:14:23.140 --> 01:14:27.650
can open up the graphical settings, pull the
ground clutter slider to 1 and remove almost

01:14:27.650 --> 01:14:32.330
all the grass in the environment, revealing
the bottles completely unobstructed.

01:14:32.330 --> 01:14:35.890
Expert practice is to manipulate the world
to the greatest extent possible in the service

01:14:35.890 --> 01:14:37.480
of instrumental play.

01:14:37.480 --> 01:14:41.191
The Potion of Inky Blackness is a cosmetic
item that shifts the player’s perception

01:14:41.191 --> 01:14:42.960
of the world from day to night.

01:14:42.960 --> 01:14:47.940
It has no overt value, but when used in specific
environments, it increases the contrast on

01:14:47.940 --> 01:14:50.730
certain visual effects, making them easier
to see.

01:14:50.730 --> 01:14:55.150
In Sepulcher of the First Ones, the potion
saw usage on 5 of the 11 bosses.

01:14:55.150 --> 01:14:58.520
The potion heavily alters the color palette
of the environment; it doesn’t necessarily

01:14:58.520 --> 01:15:02.670
look bad, but it’s a bit shonky, and certainly
not the environmental artists’ intended

01:15:02.670 --> 01:15:03.870
for the space.

01:15:03.870 --> 01:15:07.230
A long-standing practice has involved using
console commands to increase the follow distance

01:15:07.230 --> 01:15:09.560
of the ingame camera to its maximum extent.

01:15:09.560 --> 01:15:13.040
At its most extreme, it was zoomed further
back than the isometric camera of Warcraft

01:15:13.040 --> 01:15:16.890
3, the player character reduced to a barely-identifiable
speck.

01:15:16.890 --> 01:15:21.510
This God’s eye view, like in an RTS, allows
us to take in more of the play space.

01:15:21.510 --> 01:15:25.330
In 2016, Blizzard tried to reign this in and
drastically limit the maximum camera distance.

01:15:25.330 --> 01:15:30.090
As we have come to expect the players took
this in stride, adjusted their behaviour in

01:15:30.090 --> 01:15:34.920
response, and remained above all respectful
in their dealings with their fellow man.

01:15:34.920 --> 01:15:38.750
Just kidding, it was a shitshow, obviously.

01:15:38.750 --> 01:15:41.380
It was among the biggest controversies of
its kind.

01:15:41.380 --> 01:15:45.130
Blizzard, completely blindsided, eventually
backed down and only made a smaller adjustment.

01:15:45.130 --> 01:15:48.500
They limited the camera enough to annoy players
who had become accustomed to viewing the game

01:15:48.500 --> 01:15:52.900
like a World War 2 bomber pilot, but not enough
to fundamentally change the look of the game.

01:15:52.900 --> 01:15:55.440
It remains a contentious topic to this day.

01:15:55.440 --> 01:15:59.850
Blizzard’s argument for a reduced camera
was based on the nature of WoW as an RPG.

01:15:59.850 --> 01:16:02.960
They argued that players ought to feel attached
to their character, and feel like they are

01:16:02.960 --> 01:16:04.170
within the world.

01:16:04.170 --> 01:16:07.400
The difference between this, and this.

01:16:07.400 --> 01:16:14.480
[Dan] It has been observed for a long time
now that expert practice is to disable the

01:16:14.480 --> 01:16:17.980
diegetic sound, but particularly the game’s
music.

01:16:17.980 --> 01:16:21.790
Raids rely on coordination, which is often
done over voice chat software.

01:16:21.790 --> 01:16:26.041
To make sure communication is clear, players
often turn the game’s sound way down, if

01:16:26.041 --> 01:16:27.170
not off altogether.

01:16:27.170 --> 01:16:32.750
The ‘auralscape’ of the environment is
removed, before the instrumental player rebuilds

01:16:32.750 --> 01:16:33.750
it.

01:16:33.750 --> 01:16:37.980
Voice communication is one element, and then
the alerts of AddOns serve as another.

01:16:37.980 --> 01:16:42.850
The game already features audio cues for critical
events, cues lovingly crafted by designers

01:16:42.850 --> 01:16:46.380
trying to build a cohesive, subtle, thematic
experience.

01:16:46.380 --> 01:16:50.810
But if you need to make snap decisions, maybe
play it safe and replace Combustion with the

01:16:50.810 --> 01:16:52.100
wilhelm scream.

01:16:52.100 --> 01:16:56.790
In the absolute top Mythic Keystones, the
combat is so busy that all possible senses

01:16:56.790 --> 01:16:59.900
are used to the fullest possible extent.

01:16:59.900 --> 01:17:05.020
[Cacophony of audio alerts]

01:17:05.020 --> 01:17:06.830
That cacophony, that’s the soundtrack of
keystones.

01:17:06.830 --> 01:17:09.070
That’s the music of victory.

01:17:09.070 --> 01:17:11.590
[Gunshot noises]

01:17:11.590 --> 01:17:16.250
What’s really important to note is that
the music of victory isn’t the music of

01:17:16.250 --> 01:17:17.250
the game itself.

01:17:17.250 --> 01:17:21.720
It’s almost perverse the degree to which
the soundtrack, an entire pillar of the creative

01:17:21.720 --> 01:17:25.400
product, has been functionally erased from
the game for the median player, having been

01:17:25.400 --> 01:17:28.739
shut off in 2007 and never restored.

01:17:28.739 --> 01:17:33.159
All of this is about tearing down the original
soundscape of World of Warcraft and repurposing

01:17:33.159 --> 01:17:35.100
it as a means of success.

01:17:35.100 --> 01:17:39.131
Josh Keaton’s performance as Anduin Wyrnn
is muted so you can better hear a 29 year

01:17:39.131 --> 01:17:43.080
old software engineer from Montana coordinate
the most efficient movement between lanes

01:17:43.080 --> 01:17:44.080
of zombies.

01:17:44.080 --> 01:17:51.770
[Raid leader] Everyone needs to hard focus
the Monstrous Soul because we don’t have

01:17:51.770 --> 01:17:52.770
lust, so..

01:17:52.770 --> 01:17:58.130
[Choice] And with all of this established,
we can finally say the quiet part out loud.

01:17:58.130 --> 01:18:01.040
Players make World of Warcraft look fucking
ugly.

01:18:01.040 --> 01:18:04.730
Instrumental play values clarity, but only
‘clarity’ in regard to information.

01:18:04.730 --> 01:18:08.850
It’s easy for this conversation to get caught
in the quagmire of ‘personal preferences’.

01:18:08.850 --> 01:18:13.040
But these interfaces are not the product of
pure creative expression, they are the product

01:18:13.040 --> 01:18:14.550
of instrumental practices.

01:18:14.550 --> 01:18:18.280
So let’s go straight for the jugular and
highlight Mythic Archimonde as an example

01:18:18.280 --> 01:18:19.370
of what we’re talking about.

01:18:19.370 --> 01:18:22.960
Wrought Chaos was a pretty nuanced ability
but to be brief, players needed to be aware

01:18:22.960 --> 01:18:25.900
of these arrows that telegraphed an upcoming
attack.

01:18:25.900 --> 01:18:27.650
Clarity of the environment was incentivised.

01:18:27.650 --> 01:18:31.520
You can see that in how Limit positions themselves
in this footage, it provides a clarity to

01:18:31.520 --> 01:18:34.120
what is going on, even through a kind of busy
UI.

01:18:34.120 --> 01:18:42.230
There was another solution though, and it
looked like this.

01:18:42.230 --> 01:18:45.650
Players used a WeakAura that would attempt
to represent the trajectory of the beams as

01:18:45.650 --> 01:18:46.790
2 dimensional vectors.

01:18:46.790 --> 01:18:49.170
It’s pretty accurate, but not really.

01:18:49.170 --> 01:18:51.810
As a means of trivializing the mechanic, it
is highly effective.

01:18:51.810 --> 01:18:54.190
It is also objectively awful to look at.

01:18:54.190 --> 01:18:58.290
There’s no personal preference argument
here, these vectors are entirely function.

01:18:58.290 --> 01:19:02.380
The Hellfire Citadel Archimonde encounter
was already a kind of unpleasant fight visually,

01:19:02.380 --> 01:19:04.690
but players pushed it completely over the
edge.

01:19:04.690 --> 01:19:08.870
Mythic Archimonde is a sincerely ugly fight
to look back on, because it will forever be

01:19:08.870 --> 01:19:11.790
through this lens, this mess.

01:19:11.790 --> 01:19:15.080
Alex Golub called this the process of ‘decomposing
the world.’

01:19:15.080 --> 01:19:17.639
And we’ve made an art form of it.

01:19:17.639 --> 01:19:22.040
In context, Golub is using the term ‘decomposing’
as the inverse of composing, a dismantling

01:19:22.040 --> 01:19:23.040
of the world.

01:19:23.040 --> 01:19:27.350
But the imagery of decomposing, of decay,
that does feel appropriate.

01:19:27.350 --> 01:19:32.350
There is no conspiracy, no central authority,
no plan, and yet we are uncompromising and

01:19:32.350 --> 01:19:33.350
unrelenting.

01:19:33.350 --> 01:19:35.690
For almost twenty years, Azeroth has been
rotting through instrumental play and it all

01:19:35.690 --> 01:19:39.130
seems just… natural.

01:19:39.130 --> 01:19:42.730
[Dan] And if we could roll the credits there,
that would be a great ending.

01:19:42.730 --> 01:19:44.420
If only it were that simple.

01:19:44.420 --> 01:19:47.880
The relationship between instrumental play
and fun is messy.

01:19:47.880 --> 01:19:52.380
We are far from the first to observe these
issues, they aren’t exactly hard to see.

01:19:52.380 --> 01:19:57.210
A common solution put forward is to heavily
wind back, if not outright remove, the game’s

01:19:57.210 --> 01:19:58.230
AddOn support.

01:19:58.230 --> 01:20:01.600
Despite everything, that’s not a solution
we’re super crazy about.

01:20:01.600 --> 01:20:04.070
Mods offer unparalleled value to the consumer.

01:20:04.070 --> 01:20:07.960
There is a reason they were, and remain, so
beloved by PC gamers.

01:20:07.960 --> 01:20:12.540
Mod support is not solely this engine of chaos
that we’ve depicted it as, it is a means

01:20:12.540 --> 01:20:14.130
of empowering users.

01:20:14.130 --> 01:20:17.770
For that reason, the application of mods spans
far beyond what you’d expect.

01:20:17.770 --> 01:20:22.880
The Tournament of Ages, the charity roleplay
festival from earlier, is built off the back

01:20:22.880 --> 01:20:23.880
of AddOns.

01:20:23.880 --> 01:20:28.130
Disability is an enormous subject that is
so important that we weren’t sure how best

01:20:28.130 --> 01:20:29.130
to handle it.

01:20:29.130 --> 01:20:31.900
For what it’s worth, Choice doesn’t like
to make a big deal about it, but his eyes

01:20:31.900 --> 01:20:32.900
are cooked.

01:20:32.900 --> 01:20:36.840
He is very much reliant on AddOns to play
his best, and so that will always short circuit

01:20:36.840 --> 01:20:38.710
this type of conversation.

01:20:38.710 --> 01:20:43.900
Mods offer means of accessibility that are
both broad and granular, custom to every individual’s

01:20:43.900 --> 01:20:47.840
need, to a degree developers can never practically
match.

01:20:47.840 --> 01:20:53.730
MDT, an incredibly complex mod that allows
players to plan and share routes through dungeons,

01:20:53.730 --> 01:20:57.771
has dramatically altered the way that dungeons
are designed, by equipping players with the

01:20:57.771 --> 01:21:02.480
tools and language to communicate complex,
interesting, strategies.

01:21:02.480 --> 01:21:07.250
Arguably this whole process of negotiation,
the arms race between players and designers,

01:21:07.250 --> 01:21:11.440
has resulted in more replayable, engaging,
fun content.

01:21:11.440 --> 01:21:14.810
Mods are ultimately an expression of our own
behaviors.

01:21:14.810 --> 01:21:18.980
Instrumental play, the act of attempting to
solve a game, ironically can’t be solved.

01:21:18.980 --> 01:21:23.870
It’s not a puzzle box, but an expression
of values, and mods are just one of the ways

01:21:23.870 --> 01:21:25.480
in which that manifests.

01:21:25.480 --> 01:21:30.480
For all the drama that can be wrung from Golub’s
writing, his conclusion is not a tirade against

01:21:30.480 --> 01:21:32.270
mods or those who use them.

01:21:32.270 --> 01:21:37.020
Instead, Golub sees this as evidence that
virtual worlds are made compelling not by

01:21:37.020 --> 01:21:39.880
their fiction, but from their reality.

01:21:39.880 --> 01:21:44.211
World of Warcraft is the product of the emergent
collusion of developers and players, social

01:21:44.211 --> 01:21:47.820
practices go a huge way toward defining how
we got to this point.

01:21:47.820 --> 01:21:52.330
To lay the blame at the feet of, just, players
as a collective, to argue that the game would

01:21:52.330 --> 01:21:58.130
be improved if only Microsoft’s Activision-Blizzard-King
just had the control it needed to realize

01:21:58.130 --> 01:21:59.880
its creative vision.

01:21:59.880 --> 01:22:00.880
Mm.

01:22:00.880 --> 01:22:02.140
Not loving that.

01:22:02.140 --> 01:22:06.410
World of Warcraft is an emergent culture that
is constituted by its players, its inhabitants

01:22:06.410 --> 01:22:08.540
if you want to be romantic.

01:22:08.540 --> 01:22:12.040
Modern World of Warcraft is a mess in a way
that only humanity could create.

01:22:12.040 --> 01:22:16.500
It is full of contradiction and hypocrisy,
it rewards our worst impulses, before demanding

01:22:16.500 --> 01:22:17.920
payment in full.

01:22:17.920 --> 01:22:21.320
Certain anthropologists feared that WoW’s
nature as a game would undermine its value

01:22:21.320 --> 01:22:26.330
as a site for cultural study, that incentivising
behaviors would generate inauthentic data.

01:22:26.330 --> 01:22:28.230
But this feels plenty authentic to me.

01:22:28.230 --> 01:22:32.239
Golub suggests that in the face of digital
spaces we retire the concept of the “real”

01:22:32.239 --> 01:22:37.060
world, especially as the idea is posed in
opposition to the virtual, where experiences

01:22:37.060 --> 01:22:42.520
in one are true while experiences in the other
are false, fake, or fictive, that whatever

01:22:42.520 --> 01:22:45.120
happens online it’s not real.

01:22:45.120 --> 01:22:49.670
This framing neglects to encompass the very
real humans who utilize these spaces, bringing

01:22:49.670 --> 01:22:54.730
with them all their human attributes, their
drama, the tangle of their lives.

01:22:54.730 --> 01:22:58.670
Virtual spaces are not apart from the world
but an extension of it, and to this Golub

01:22:58.670 --> 01:23:04.380
suggests a much tamer distinction between
the virtual world and the actual world, demoting

01:23:04.380 --> 01:23:07.060
the physical to a mere subset of reality.

01:23:07.060 --> 01:23:11.670
“The key feature of the actual world which
virtual worlds must share with it in order

01:23:11.670 --> 01:23:15.710
to become compelling is not its visual and
sonic “realism,” but the fact it is a

01:23:15.710 --> 01:23:20.230
forum in which we give our lives meaning by
entangling them in projects we undertake with

01:23:20.230 --> 01:23:21.230
others.

01:23:21.230 --> 01:23:25.630
Worlds become real when we care about them,
not when they look similar to our own”.

